 Russia's gross domestic product may grow by a "very weak" 3.5% to 4% this year due to a "stagnant" first half and the effects of this summer's drought and forest fires, Raiffeisen Bank says. "Economic indicators in the last few months are giving a pretty contradictory picture: strong growth in consumer demand is happening on a background of wilting salaries and falling investment." The government expects the economy to grow by around 4% this year. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1304 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD bouces back to 1.2700 after hitting intraday low at 1.2678 in NY, still way down from 1.2820 late Thu, according to EBS via CQG. Investors abandoning EUR on the heels of ECB Weber's comments that policy tightening should be delayed to 2011. Investors also turning backs on riskier assets over continued concerns that US growth is slowing. "We have been noting that the euro has struggled to sustain rebounds even given the recent positive news from Europe [successful bond issues], which suggests that the euro is now vulnerable as the news turns less favourable," say \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  analysts.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (bradley.davis@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1241 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed funds futures all but given up on prospect of a rise in the Fed's benchmark rate early next summer. July 2011 contract recently up 2 BPs at 99.735, pricing in a 6% likelihood of rate hike at late June meeting of the FOMC. Slim as it is, that's an increase from early Thu, when traders wiped out any expectation for a rate rise after dire readings on unemployment and Philly Fed's business activity report. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1240 GMT [Dow Jones] Eurodollar futures prices edge higher just after mkt open Friday, reflecting expectations for key inter-bank lending rates to creep higher in the months ahead. Front-month Sep contract up 1.5 BPs at 99.667, envisioning three-month U.S. dollar Libor at 0.333% by contract expiration, a slight lift off current 0.32922% fixing. Dec Eurodollars, up 1 BP at 99.61, see Libor at 0.39% by year-end. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasury prices broadly higher in early trade Friday. With nothing on the economic data calendar and with no Fed speakers on deck, "the markets have been left to their own devices and this has resulted in a continuation of yesterday's trends" with investors still flocking to safer assets, says RBS. The 30-year leads with the price up 20/32 to yield 3.624%; 10-year up 6/32 to yield 2.555% and 5-year up 2/32 to yield 1.38%. (kejal.vyas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] EUROPEAN SUMMARY: What promised to be a dull session with no data or events on tap turned out to be anything but. It started with European government bonds breaking to new highs over persisting worries over the health of the global economy. Comments on Bloomberg from Bundesbank president Weber that the ECB should contemplate exit strategies in early 2011 but keep liquidity cash tenders in place until then were seen as dovish from the usual uber-hawk. Yields plummeted across the board and the euro took a hit. The 10-year bund futures contract printed a record high of 133.02, 10-year cash yield fell to a record low of 2.236%, 30-year a record low 2.898%. In the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets the euro fell across the board with EUR/USD at a one month low of 1.2685 from 1.2833, EUR/JPY and EUR/CHF both at fresh 7 week lows of 108.25 and 1.3140 respectively. GBP/USD was dragged down by the euro shedding one cent to a 3-week low 1.5465 while USD/JPY stayed out of the fracas trading between 85.28-48. Elsewhere September gilts hit a contract high of 125.23 and the U.S. 2 yr Treasury note yield fell to a record low of 0.463%. The main European bourses are down around 0.5%, gold is flat at $1231oz and oil is trading just above $74bbl. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) [ 08-20-1