\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Russian Capital Controls Less Likely Now - RBC\b0\par\par\uc2 1,303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2010\par 15:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1353 GMT [Dow Jones] Russia is less likely to enact capital controls now, given the less stable external backdrop and negative net private capital flow, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says. Nevertheless, "continued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is costly, and reduced inflows would lessen that cost." Any capital controls would probably be targeted, rather than broad based, the bank says. These may include high tax penalties for withdrawing portfolio investments early, differentiated reserve requirements for banks, caps on foreign borrowing by state-owned companies, and reducing the tax-deductibility of interest payments on foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1348 GMT [Dow Jones] The major theme in recent weeks is global investors gravitating to emerging market local bonds, says RBS. Month-to-date, 2-year through 10-year yields have fallen between 5 bps and 50 bps across key liquid markets, such as Brazil, Mexico, Poland, South Korea and South Africa. Only Turkey is against trend. This comes as (1) investors flock from stocks on global growth fears;(2) EM local bonds have higher yields than most of the world; (3) many developed markets look to be on cusp of recession; and (4) there is some evidence China is buying to diversify \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, says RBS. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1329 GMT [Dow Jones] Emerging market bonds are weaker on the day, with broader market moves, as investors pare riskier positions. The risk premium on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's Emerging Markets Bond Index Global is 1 bp wider to 300 bps over Tsys. The index is down 0.10%. Brazil's benchmark bond, the Global 2040, is down 1/8 to 138 3/4 bid, according to Reuters. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1327 GMT [Dow Jones] The overnight indexed swaps market is pricing in virtually even odds of another 25-basis-point rate raise from the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  at its next policy date on Sept 8 after soft Canadian CPI data for July. That's down from a more than 60% chance ahead of the data. One month of softer CPI data won't convince the bank that inflationary pressures are receding on a sustained basis, suggesting that 2Q GDP data to be released on Aug 31, the last important release before the decision date, will be a key input for the bank, one Toronto economist said. "It's really upped the ante on that figure," he said. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1325 GMT [Dow Jones] Sep-dated 10-yr Tsy note futures prices turn negative, trading near session lows ahead of US stock mkt open Friday, though equity index futures continue to point to a lower open. 10-yr note futures recently down 2/32 at 125-28+, as investors ease off earlier bid following heavy round of buying Thursday. Sep 30-yr bond futures pare earlier gains, recently up 6/32 at 134-17. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1309 GMT [Dow Jones] Russia's gross domestic product may grow by a "very weak" 3.5% to 4% this year due to a "stagnant" first half and the effects of this summer's drought and forest fires, Raiffeisen Bank says. "Economic indicators in the last few months are giving a pretty contradictory picture: strong growth in consumer demand is happening on a background of wilting salaries and falling investment." The government expects the economy to grow by around 4% this year. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1304 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD bouces back to 1.2700 after hitting intraday low at 1.2678 in NY, still way down from 1.2820 late Thu, according to EBS via CQG. Investors abandoning EUR on the heels of ECB Weber's comments that policy tightening should be delayed to 2011. Investors also turning backs on riskier assets over continued concerns that US growth is slowing. "We have been noting that the euro has struggled to sustain rebounds even given the recent positive news from Europe [successful bond issues], which suggests that the euro is now vulnerable as the news turns less favourable," say \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  analysts.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (bradley.davis@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1241 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed funds futures all but given up on prospect of a rise in the Fed's benchmark rate early next summer. July 2011 contract recently up 2 BPs at 99.735, pricing in a 6% likelihood of rate hike at late June meeting of the FOMC. Slim as it is, that's an increase from early Thu, when traders wiped out any expectation for a rate rise after dire readings on unemployment and Philly Fed's business activity report. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1240 GMT [Dow Jones] Eurodollar futures prices edge higher just after mkt open Friday, reflecting expectations for key inter-bank lending rates to creep higher in the months ahead. Front-month Sep contract up 1.5 BPs at 99.667, envisioning three-month U.S. dollar Libor at 0.333% by contract expiration, a slight lift off current 0.32922% fixing. Dec Eurodollars, up 1 BP at 99.61, see Libor at 0.39% by year-end. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasury prices broadly higher in early trade Friday. With nothing on the economic data calendar and with no Fed speakers on deck, "the markets have been left to their own devices and this has resulted in a continuation of yesterday's trends" with investors still flocking to safer assets, says RBS. The 30-year leads with the price up 20/32 to yield 3.624%; 10-year up 6/32 to yield 2.555% and 5-year up 2/32 to yield 1.38%. (kejal.vyas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] EUROPEAN SUMMARY: What promised to be a dull session with no data or events on tap turned out to be anything but. It started with European government bonds breaking to new highs over persisting worries over the health of the global economy. Comments on Bloomberg from Bundesbank president Weber that the ECB should contemplate exit strategies in early 2011 but keep liquidity cash tenders in place until then were seen as dovish from the usual uber-hawk. Yields plummeted across the board and the euro took a hit. The 10-year bund futures contract printed a record high of 133.02, 10-year cash yield fell to a record low of 2.236%, 30-year a record low 2.898%. In the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets the euro fell across the board with EUR/USD at a one month low of 1.2685 from 1.2833, EUR/JPY and EUR/CHF both at fresh 7 week lows of 108.25 and 1.3140 respectively. GBP/USD was dragged down by the euro shedding one cent to a 3-week low 1.5465 while USD/JPY stayed out of the fracas trading between 85.28-48. Elsewhere September gilts hit a contract high of 125.23 and the U.S. 2 yr Treasury note yield fell to a record low of 0.463%. The main European bourses are down around 0.5%, gold is flat at $1231oz and oil is trading just above $74bbl. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1234 GMT [Dow Jones] While there are signs Poland's economic recovery is losing momentum, the Monetary Policy Council will not be in hurry to tighten monetary policy, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. "we would see one 25 basis point hike within the next twelve months - likely in the second half of 2011," Dankse says. "Even that hike could 'disappear,'" if growth were to slow more in the coming months, Danske says. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com) [ 08-20-10 0953ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100820e68k0008k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
