\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ BOC WATCH: September Rate Raise In Question After Fed Policy Shift\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \uc2 790 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2010\par 20:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Can the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  keep tightening while the U.S. Federal Reserve aims at staying loose?\par \par \uc2 The Fed's discrete retreat even further from monetary tightening last week has some observers thinking the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  may have to curtail its own tightening cycle. Given how closely the two countries' economies are intertwined, it's hard for the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pursue a radically different policy track for very long, they argue.\par \par \uc2 Others believe some key aspects of the two countries' economies have diverged so markedly the bank has the ability to continue tightening policy independently of the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The market appears to be taking the first view into account. Tuesday morning, the overnight-indexed swaps market was pricing in about a 60% chance of a rate increase at the bank's next announcement date on Sept. 8, down from the near certainty discounted earlier in August. The decline also reflects some weak Canadian and U.S. data in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  period.\par \par \uc2 Expectations for the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been more volatile than usual in recent weeks in part because markets know the bank is acutely sensitive to both external and domestic developments.\par \par \uc2 The bank has raised its key overnight target rate by 50 basis points since June, and remains the only \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  among the G7 group of industrialized economies to embark on tightening in the period following the global financial market crisis in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Since launching its tightening, the bank has underscored what a cautious and context-sensitive tightening it is. The question now is how the Fed's reassertion of monetary easing alters the context for the bank.\par \par \uc2 One reason that policy cannot diverge from the Fed's on a sustained basis is the Canadian dollar's acute sensitivity to interest rate differentials between the U.S. and Canada. If the bank continues to raise rates while the Fed abstains, the Canadian dollar will likely appreciate. In a trade-oriented economy like Canada's, the net result is monetary tightening that could easily exceed what the bank would like.\par \par \uc2 Another is that the close ties between the Canadian and U.S. economies mean the renewed weakness the Fed is worried about could easily spread to Canada.\par \par \uc2 "If we do get into a much weaker-than-expected U.S. environment, that is going to spill over into Canada one way or another," said Doug Porter, deputy chief economist at BMO Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 Housing is one sector where Canada had clearly diverged from the U.S., with Canada's market shutting down for a few months immediately after the global financial crisis and then picking up steam again through 2009 and into this year. In recent months, however, it's been sputtering.\par \par \uc2 "We've got pretty clear evidence that housing has stalled, and the best days are behind it, already," said BMO's Porter.\par \par \uc2 While the gap between the U.S. and Canadian housing sectors has narrowed somewhat, another key divergence remains. Consumer borrowing in Canada has remained healthy. Business borrowing has as well, although to a lesser degree.\par \par \uc2 A recent report from Scotia Capital said that's the single biggest reason the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  can part company sustainably with the Fed and stay on a tightening track.\par \par \uc2 While the U.S. is deleveraging and stuck in a liquidity trap that means monetary policy is not working to stimulate demand side of the picture, Canada is leveraging and monetary policy is arguably working too well, Scotia said.\par \par \uc2 Within six months, Canadian households will be more heavily indebted than Americans on a debt-to-income basis, Scotia says.\par \par \uc2 "The BOC has to be concerned about facing the same tag applied to the Fed in keeping rates too low for too long when looking out at risks over the coming years," Scotia said.\par \par \uc2 "Yes, the uncertainty has risen. But the fundamentals are still supportive of a continued pace of tightening," it said.\par \par \uc2 (Don Curren, a reporter based in Toronto, has covered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income markets for more than 10 years. He can be reached at 416-306-2020 or at don.curren@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 17, 2010 14:00 ET (18:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100817e68h0044o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
