\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Afternoon: C$ Up, Hits 19-Month High After Jobs Data\b0\par\par\uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 21:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar ended significantly higher Friday, breaking out of its well-defined trading range and hitting its highest level in 19 months after an unexpectedly robust jobs report for February.\par \par \uc2 The rally opened the door for a run to parity with the U.S. dollar, possibly in the near term, market watchers said.\par \par \uc2 "I think that the Canadian dollar is really convincingly moving to par, now," said Aaron Fennell, senior market strategist at futures broker \uc2 Lind-Waldock\uc2  Canada in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.0185 at 3:13 p.m. EST (2013 GMT), from C$1.0171 at 8:00 a.m. EST (1300 GMT), and from C$1.0246 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar dropped to a daily low at C$1.0157, its lowest level since July 25, 2008, after news in early trading that the Canadian economy created 20,900 jobs in February, stronger than the 17,000 expected by economists and the unemployment rate unexpectedly fell to 8.2% from 8.3% in the previous month.\par \par \uc2 The drop took the U.S. unit through key support near C$1.0200, breaking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair of out the C$1.0200 to C$1.0850 range that has held since October and setting the stage for a move to parity.\par \par \uc2 "The move we're seeing today is just directly off the labor survey, which was fantastic," said \uc2 Lind-Waldock\uc2 's Fennell.\par \par \uc2 "I think the key to it was we had 60,000 full-time jobs and a loss of 39,000 part-time. That just means a lot of part-time jobs are being changed to full-time," Fennell said. "I think that's a fantastic signal for the Canadian economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 Fennell believes there will be at least one correction in the U.S./Canadian dollar pair as it descends towards parity.\par \par \uc2 "I would expect as we approach par there will be a lot of profit taking," he said. "I think it will take one or two runs at par before it finally goes through," Fennell said.\par \par \uc2 A report from \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  said that while the pair gapped under the C$1.0170/C$1.0200 support zone Friday morning, there is not a strong sense, at the moment at least, that it is running away on the downside.\par \par \uc2 "Downside risks are certainly higher this morning, and certainly higher than we had expected at this point yesterday, but the USD is looking very oversold already," TD said.\par \par \uc2 But technical indicators suggest the U.S. dollar can only rebound more significantly if spot moves above C$1.0220 level, TD added.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar is still vulnerable to bouts of risk aversion in global markets, but any losses due to that factor will likely be transitory, said \uc2 Lind-Waldock\uc2 's Fennell.\par \par \uc2 When the Canadian dollar appeared to be gaining momentum for a push to parity last fall, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally in the market, with considerable success, to quell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains.\par \par \uc2 Fennell and many other market watchers believe Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be more restrained in the coming weeks, reflecting the fact that the export sector has had time to adjust to Canadian dollar strength.\par \par \uc2 "Any manufacturer or exporter who would have problems with higher Canadian dollar values has had ample opportunities to set up hedges," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian data calendar next week is characterized by a number of second-tier reports, including labor productivity for the fourth quarter and manufacturing sales for January on Tuesday, wholesale trade for January on Wednesday and international securities transactions for the same month on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The most significant releases come Friday, when consumer price index data for February and retail sales data for January will be released.\par \par \uc2 These are the exchange rates at 3:13 p.m. EST (2013 GMT), 8:00 a.m. EST (1300 GMT), and late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    USD/CAD     1.0185      1.0171     1.0246 \par    EUR/CAD     1.4013      1.4010     1.4015 \par    CAD/JPY      88.91       88.96      88.44 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020; don.curren@dowjones.com [ 03-12-10 1517ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1158   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100312e63c000bn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
