\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: The Loonie Will Likely Take Off Again\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2010\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  may well need to clip the Canadian dollar's wings more if it wants to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising.\par \par \uc2 If the bank doesn't, the loonie dollar could make it back up to parity against its U.S. counterpart in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 The last time the loonie made a sustained break of that level was back in May 2008.\par \par \uc2 See the Canadian dollar's recent gains against the U.S. dollar\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/3310"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/3310 }}}\par \par \uc2 At the moment, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under a little pressure -- hurt like other commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by talk that China is putting its foot on the economic brake to slow its rampant growth.\par \par \uc2 Fear that demand from China itself, as well as demand from other economies that depend on Chinese growth, will fall has helped to dampen recent investor interest in the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  also helped to remove some support when its policy meeting this week proved to be even less eventful than forecast.\par \par \uc2 The market had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave rates unchanged at 0.25% but some analysts had been looking for it to be a little more hawkish and suggest that rates could be lifted before the second half of the year.\par \par \uc2 In the event, the bank didn't. Its statement on the outlook was as dovish as the last one in December.\par \par \uc2 However, the bank did appear to be a little more tolerant of the Canadian dollar's recent rise. The bank repeated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's persistent strength puts a drag on the economy but it failed to be any more critical than it was in its December statement, even though the U.S. dollar has fallen about 3% against the loonie since then.\par \par \uc2 In December, the bank downgraded its opposition to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "We believe this signals increased confidence in the ability of the Canadian economy to weather \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  'strength' and may indicate that the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's 'pain threshold' on U.S. dollar/Canadian dollar is drifting lower," said Todd Elmer, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's caution on the economy, some analysts are starting to bet that the first hike in interest rates will come sooner than expected as the Canadian economy continues to recover.\par \par \uc2 Much could depend on just how quickly inflation pressure starts to pick up. The latest price data on Wednesday showed annual consumer price rises accelerating to 1.3% in December from 1.0% in November.\par \par \uc2 Some market participants think that the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  could still resort to market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any rally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Others reckon that with rates down at 0.25%, the loonie could well prove popular as a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as carry trades that favor \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher yields return.\par \par \uc2 Chances are that the loonie, which has steadily pushed the U.S. dollar down from nearly CAD1.28 a year or so ago, will continue pushing ahead, especially if central banks elsewhere start looking at it as a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now that both the U.S. dollar and the debt-troubled euro are falling out of favor.\par \par \uc2 Early Thursday in Europe, the dollar was a little lower at CAD1.0471 from CAD1.0478 late on Wednesday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was mixed elsewhere as well as news that China's GDP grew by 10.7% in the fourth quarter of last year, up from 8.9% in the previous quarter, and that inflation rose to 1.9% from 1.7%, which left investors concerned that the country will soon slam on the monetary brakes to prevent overheating.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was up at Y91.56 from Y91.23, while the euro was down a little at $1.4090 from $1.4102. The euro was also up at Y129.06 from Y128.69.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for over 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 21-01-10 0815GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI00000201001