\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Pares Losses As Dubai Debt Fears Remain\b0\par\par\uc2 By Fabio Alves   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 876 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2009\par 15:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar recovered some of its earlier losses Monday morning in New York as investors continue to fret over Dubai's debt crisis despite the United Arab Emirates' move to provide funds to local banks.\par \par \uc2 While safe-haven buying has once again lent support to the greenback, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains down on the day versus most major rivals.\par \par \uc2 The greenback will likely take further cues from U.S. stocks, as weaker equity prices tend to erode demand for riskier assets, including the euro and other higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Futures on the Standard & Poor's 500 index indicate a slightly lower open.\par \par \uc2 In Monday morning trading, the euro was trading at $1.5010, from $1.4955 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y86.46, from Y86.75, while the euro was at Y129.80, from Y129.71. The U.K. pound was at $1.6470, from $1.6487. The dollar was at CHF1.0037, from CHF1.0064.\par \par \uc2 The Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of six \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 74.774 from 75.034.\par \par \uc2 European equities have turned lower as caution overtook optimism after statements from a Dubai Finance Ministry official indicated that state-run \uc2 Dubai World\uc2 's liabilities aren't guaranteed by the government.\par \par \uc2 "There's still concern that (Dubai's debt problems) could spiral into something worse, so you have the domino effect," said Jacob Oubina, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com in Bedminster, N.J.\par \par \uc2 "As long as stocks remain in negative terrain in New York, the euro will continue to struggle with the $1.51" level, he said.\par \par \uc2 The euro rose as far as $1.5085 during Asian trading in the wake of news that the UAE's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will provide funds to banks with exposure to state-run \uc2 Dubai World\uc2 's debt, after the conglomerate announced last week a six-month payment standstill on its debt.\par \par \uc2 Dubai World\uc2 , an investment company in property and financial services, accounts for about $60 billion of the city-state's $80 billion in liabilities.\par \par \uc2 Dubai and Abu Dhabi are two of the seven emirates that make up the U.A.E. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is mainly backed by Abu Dhabi.\par \par \uc2 News of the UAE's financial support to Dubai's banks "does little to convince foreign bond holders that they're not going to take a hit," said Chris Turner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at ING Financial Markets in London.\par \par \uc2 After stocks in Asia rose on improved sentiment, European equities fell into the red as concerns increased over Dubai's debt problems after statements made by Abdulrahman Al Saleh, director general of Dubai's Department of Finance to state-run Dubai TV Monday.\par \par \uc2 "The company received financing based on its project schedule, not a government guarantee...they [the lenders] have deemed \uc2 Dubai World\uc2  as part of the government and this is not true," Al Saleh said.\par \par \uc2 Losses in European bourses "imply ongoing jitters with the Dubai fallout, especially as U.K. banks have yet to unveil their exposure to the Gulf markets," said Ashraf Laidi, chief strategist at CMC Markets in London.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar recovered some of its earlier losses against the yen after Japanese Strategy Minister Naoto Kan said the government had agreed to measures to stop the yen's rise. Kan didn't specify what measures would be used.\par \par \uc2 The greenback had fallen as far as Y85.96 during Asian trading.\par \par \uc2 Also, Japanese Finance Minister Hirohisa Fujii Monday denied saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was unthinkable for the time being.\par \par \uc2 "I never said anything like that," Fujii told a group of reporters.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, a report in the Mainichi Shimbun quoted Fujii as saying: "(We're) now at the stage of monitoring the (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) situations and (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is unthinkable."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Morning \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian dollar is modestly higher Monday morning, reflecting the general, moderate retreat by the U.S. dollar. The U.S. dollar is trading at C$1.0598 from C$1.0612 late Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian dollar retreated slightly after Statistics Canada reported the economy emerged from the first recession in almost 20 years in the third quarter, though the recovery was slower than expected and lagged behind the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 StatsCan said gross domestic product expanded an annualized 0.4% in the third quarter after three consecutive declines, weaker than the expected 1.0% gain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BMO Capital Markets said liquidity in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to return to more normal levels after last week's diminished liquidity contributed to fairly volatile treading conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday's price action is likely to be determined by month-end flows, BMO said. The short to medium-term outlook for the pair has it confined to roughly a C$1.0425 to C$1.0750 range, BMO added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Fabio Alves, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2204;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 fabio.alves@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Don Curren in Toronto, Nicholas Hastings in London, Summer Said in Cairo and Takashi Mochizuki in Tokyo contributed to this article.) [ 11-30-09 0930ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091130e5bu0009t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
