\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  needs to look at  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  in extreme circumstances to keep loonie from over appreciating: \uc2 CIBC World Markets Inc\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2009\par 12:30\par \uc2 Canada NewsWire\par \uc2 CNNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9  2009  Canada NewsWire Ltd. All rights reserved.      \par \par \uc2 \f2 TORONTO, Oct. 27 /CNW/ -- \par     <<\par     Over inflated Canadian dollar risks hollowing out the country's\par     manufacturing base\par     >>\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TORONTO, Oct. 27 /CNW/ - The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should look to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at extreme times to keep the value of the loonie stable and protect Canada's manufacturing base, finds a new report from CIBC World Markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The report argues that by not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when speculators are pushing the dollar to heights it does not believe are fundamentally sound, the Bank risks a hollowing out of the country's industrial base.\par \par \uc2 "Canada could consider what might be called a bounded float," says CIBC's Chief Economist Avery Shenfeld in the bank's latest Economic Insights report. "By \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  only at extremes, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help chase away speculative flows that take the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in its considered judgment, beyond fundamentally driven levels."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shenfeld notes that Canada has let markets decide where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should trade since 1998, effectively leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tool to gather dust in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's basement. The policy of the Bank is not to target the exchange rate, but rather to take any net stimulus or drag associated with an under- or overvalued loonie into account in setting interest rates. He adds that in the current environment, that means leaving interest rates at rock bottom for longer than would otherwise be the case.\par \par \uc2 "Anyone who has watched the Canadian dollar's performance in the last 15 years would have a tough time arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are the perfectly rational, calculating machines that the textbooks suggest," says Mr. Shenfeld. "Clearly, it's not simply trade flows and commodity prices, but also wildly fluctuating expectations and the hot money flows they generate, that set the tone for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  admits as much when it points out the dangers of recent Canadian dollar appreciation."\par \par \uc2 One of the concerns in not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the rapid appreciation of the Canadian dollar is that the Canadian economy is made up of many different sectors that react very differently to changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values and interest rates. "If one thinks of GDP as simply an undifferentiated lump of output, then \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  bubbles would not seem to be too consequential for Canada," adds Mr. Shenfeld.\par \par \uc2 "In the current context, the drag on overall growth and inflation could be largely offset by the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's decision to keep rates on hold for an extended period. This month's interest rate announcement sent a clear message that to the extent that the Canadian dollar will delay re-attaining the two per cent inflation target, it will help keep rates low for longer, giving an offsetting boost to output."\par \par \uc2 While this may keep the country's GDP numbers on an upward slope, it's the composition of growth that concerns Mr. Shenfeld. "In the real world, the mix of output also matters. Putting people into larger houses because mortgage rates are lower than otherwise generates economic output in the construction industry, and leaves the economy with a larger stock of housing.\par \par \uc2 "But if the loonie is overvalued for a few years, we may be sacrificing business plant and equipment on the altar of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Plants that close because they are unprofitable at current exchange rates might permanently relocate elsewhere. They won't suddenly come back if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later cheapens. We are hollowing out our industrial base by letting speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market forces, in effect, dictate the mix of monetary conditions."\par \par \uc2 Given this, he believes it may be time for the Bank to shift policy gears and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when it believes the loonie is overvalued. "To those who say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  never works, China has steered the Yuan exactly as it pleases, while Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year stopped the franc's appreciation against the euro dead in its tracks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a powerful tool for those who opt to use it."\par \par \uc2 What he doesn't want to see is a fixed exchange rate, "which forever hands over our interest rate policy to the (U.S.) Federal Reserve (Board), and fails to allow any adjustment to longer swings in commodity prices."\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     <<\par     The complete CIBC World Markets report is available at:\par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://research.cibcwm.com/economic_public/download/soct09.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://research.cibcwm.com/economic_public/download/soct09.pdf}}}\uc2 \f2 .\par     >>\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CIBC's wholesale banking business provides a range of integrated credit and capital markets products, investment banking, and merchant banking to clients in key financial markets in North America and around the world. We provide innovative capital solutions and advisory expertise across a wide range of industries as well as top-ranked research for our corporate, government and institutional clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CIBC World Markets Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . is a wholly-owned subsidiary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CM: TSX; NYSE) and part of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce\f28 \uc2 \f2 's wholesale banking arm which also includes other affiliates including: \f28 \uc2 \f2 CIBC World Markets Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., CIBC World Markets plc, CIBC World Markets Securities Ireland Limited, CIBC Australia Ltd, and CIBC World Markets (Japan) Inc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Avery Shenfeld, Chief Economist, CIBC World Markets Inc. at (416) 594-7356, avery.shenfeld@cibc.ca; or Kevin Dove, Communications and Public Affairs at (416) 980-8835, kevin.dove@cibc.ca   \par \par \uc2 200910270730CANADANWCANADAPR_C2763.xml   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CNNW000020091027e5ar0018k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
