\par\par \b \uc2 CANADA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  DEBT-C$ nears 3-week low as commodities, stocks fall\b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2009\par 12:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * C$ eases more than 1 cent to C$1.0698 to the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 * Stock reversal, commodity drop drive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower\par \par \uc2 * Canadian bonds mostly weaker, but outperform U.S.\par \par \uc2 By Frank Pingue and Jeffrey Hodgson\par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar fell to its lowest level in nearly three weeks Monday, as a drop in commodity prices and retreating global stocks added to losses triggered by recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 North America stocks had initially opened higher, but reversed direction as commodity prices fell and risk aversion increased, prompting investors to look to the safety of the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The whole world got very unsafe very quickly this morning," said Steve Butler, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Scotia Capital.\par \par \uc2 "Stock markets turned around very dramatically and in the same breath the U.S. dollar turned around very dramatically and so we've seen a big shoot-up in the U.S. dollar and it's hurt almost every \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... Canada was no exception."\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar often trades based on the risk appetite of global investors and prospects for the country's commodity exports.\par \par \uc2 Oil fell more than 2 percent to below $79 a barrel on concerns that a sluggish economic recovery will keep global demand low. Gold also hit a 10-day trough.\par \par \uc2 At mid-afternoon, the Canadian dollar fell to C$1.0698 to the U.S. dollar, or 93.48 U.S. cents, its lowest since Oct. 6, and down from C$1.0519 to the U.S. dollar, or 95.07 U.S. cents, at Friday's close.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had already taken a hit last week after the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  and \uc2 Governor Mark Carney\uc2  warned that a rally toward parity with the greenback was a risk to growth and mentioned the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Obviously, Carney's comments and the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement have taken a little bit of wind out of the sail of the Canadian dollar near term," said Firas Askari, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at BMO Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 Carney echoed the comments in a speech in Montreal on Monday, repeating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's view that the current strength in the Canadian dollar would more than fully offset favorable economic developments since July.\par \par \uc2 Canadian government bond prices, with no domestic data to consider until later this week, were mostly weaker, mirroring losses in U.S. Treasury bonds, which investors sold ahead of a record round of new debt auctions.\par \par \uc2 The two-year Canadian government bond fell 1 Canadian cent to C$99.42 to yield 1.533 percent, while the 10-year bond fell 30 Canadian cents to C$101.65 to yield 3.545 percent.\par \par \uc2 Canadian bonds mostly outperformed U.S. issues, with the 10-year yield 3 basis points below its U.S. counterpart, compared with 2 basis points above on Friday. (Editing by Rob Wilson)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CANADA/DOLLAR-BONDS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091029e5aq001hv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
