\par\par \b \uc2 CANADA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  DEBT-C$ lower as BoC warnings on strength linger\b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2009\par 13:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * C$ falls to overnight low of C$1.0535 to the US$\par \par \uc2 * Bond prices pinned lower across the curve\par \par \uc2 By Frank Pingue\par \par \uc2 TORONTO, Oct 23 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar fell versus the U.S. greenback on Friday as another warning from the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  in the previous session that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally is a risk to growth continued to weigh on sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier this week a surging Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was undermining Canada's economic recovery, which killed thoughts of an early interest rate hike and raised some concerns the bank might signal it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, \uc2 Governor Mark Carney\uc2  said in a news conference that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was an option but also stressed the bank's main concern was controlling inflation. [ID:nN22502163]\par \par \uc2 The comments sent Canada's dollar to a slightly lower close on Thursday, and the sentiment carried on through the overnight session where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as low as C$1.0535 to the U.S. dollar, or 94.92 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 "It's definitely underperforming against most of the crosses," said David Bradley, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Scotia Capital in Toronto. "Just a follow through from the comments made by Carney and the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  over the last couple of days."\par \par \uc2 Bradley also said the Canadian dollar would have to rally through its 2007 high when it topped US$1.10 to convince the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 7:40 a.m. (1140 GMT), the Canadian unit was at C$1.0530 to the U.S. dollar, or 94.97 U.S. cents, down from C$1.0478 to the U.S. dollar, or 95.44 U.S. cents, at Thursday's close.\par \par \uc2 Domestic bond prices, with no Canadian data to influence a move, were pinned lower across the curve alongside the bigger U.S. Treasury as overseas equities rallied and sapped demand for more secure government debt. (Editing by Padraic Cassidy)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CANADA/DOLLAR-BONDS|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|U|RBN|CANT|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091026e5an0013m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
