\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ BOC WATCH:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Keeps Policy Toolkit Wide Open\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2009\par 21:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  has made clear its determination to use all the tools at its disposal to achieve its goal of bringing inflation back to its 2% target as the strong Canadian dollar threatens to impede the nation's fragile recovery.\par \par \uc2 That's a key message from the bank's monetary policy report published Thursday, which fleshed out the steady policy stance affirmed by the bank earlier this week, but also made clear the bank still has a lot of flexibility when it comes to policy.\par \par \uc2 It also dispels any notion that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could back away from its commitment to keep its benchmark rate at 0.25% until the end of June 2010.\par \par \uc2 Although not specifically mentioned in the report, the bank's toolkit does include \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That was made clear in the subsequent news conference by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Mark Carney, who said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "always an option."\par \par \uc2 The bank hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the Canadian dollar since 1998, despite the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rush to well above parity with the U.S. dollar in late 2007, though it did participate in the 2000 round of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro. That suggests it would be very reluctant to use that tool. On the other hand, as Carney made clear Thursday morning, it can never be completely ruled out.\par \par \uc2 Carney said the bank has been unusually transparent in laying out what it judges to be the most appropriate path to achieving the inflation target: its conditional pledge to keep the overnight target rate at 0.25% until the second half of next year.\par \par \uc2 But if it has to change that, it will. "Depending on the sum of the events that happen going forward, we will adjust policy as required to achieve that target, and markets shouldn't underestimate that," he said.\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely, some form of quantitative or credit easing as laid out by the bank in April looks to be the more likely response if the Canadian dollar's negative impact on the economy exceeds the bank's fears.\par \par \uc2 But the exchange rate is only one variable among many that affect the economy. What's important are the ultimate results in terms of growth and inflation, and that's what the bank seeks to influence through its policy tools.\par \par \uc2 As things stand now, the bank sees the risks against its forecast of inflation returning to target in the third quarter of 2011 as tilting slightly to the down side. That's only because if it was forced to adopt unconventional easing policies, it would have to do so prudently and that might keep inflation lower than target.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting reasoning, and it's clearly not that of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has definitively moved beyond the possibility of some form of quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 But it's also important to remember that the bank sees the underlying macroeconomic risks as balanced, and from where it sits now, the bank's preferred course would be to keep rates at their lowest possible level until halfway through next year, in accordance with its conditional commitment.\par \par \uc2 (Don Curren, a reporter in Toronto, has covered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed-income markets for more than 10 years. He can be reached at 416-306-2020 or at don.curren@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 22, 2009 15:51 ET (19:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020091022e5am00667\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
