\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE:BOC's Carney Says  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  "Always An Option"\b0\par\par\uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2009\par 19:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout with more detail.)\par \par \uc2 OTTAWA (Dow Jones)--Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Mark Carney said Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is "always an option" to lower the value of the Canadian dollar, and markets shouldn't underestimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to set policy to achieve the 2% inflation target.\par \par \uc2 Carney made the comments in a news conference after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released its October Monetary Policy Report.\par \par \uc2 "Markets should take seriously our determination to set policy to achieve the inflation target. Markets sometimes lose their focus, we don't lose our focus," Carney said. "We will deploy the tools at our disposal appropriately in order the achieve the inflation target." He added that the Bank retains "considerable flexibility" to conduct monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 He said the persistently high Canadian dollar is a major downside risk to the economy and, because of the lag effect, the impact will be especially felt in 2010 and 2011, particularly the latter year.\par \par \uc2 "It's a risk, it's more than offset the positive developments that have happened since July, which have been considerable," he said. "It is the major downside risk - continued persistence of the (Canadian) dollar."\par \par \uc2 If not for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's spike, the Bank's new forecasts would have been higher than in July, he said.\par \par \uc2 Although the story of the strong Canadian dollar isn't new - the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly reached parity with the U.S. unit in late 2007 - the situation this time is different, Carney said. The global economy is emerging from a deep recession and recovery is expected to be modest.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think we should downplay the scale of the challenge just because we've been at this level before," he said. "It's a very different situation today."\par \par \uc2 Carney repeated the Bank's commitment to keep the overnight rate at a record low 0.25% to the end of June 2010 provided inflation remains tame.\par \par \uc2 He said consumer demand will be even more at the heart of the economic recovery than expected in July, partly because the strong Canadian dollar makes exports more expensive.\par \par \uc2 He said there's a "tremendous amount of slack" in the economy which will offset inflationary pressures. The headline and core consumer price index are both now forecast to return to 2% only in the third quarter of 2011, three months later than predicted in July.\par \par \uc2 Carney said there are few parallels between Canada and Australia, which recently became the first G20 country to hike rates.\par \par \uc2 Among other things, Canada's economy is more open, it's emerging from a deep recession, and its biggest trading partner is the U.S. Australia, by contrast, didn't have a recession and its largest trading partner is China. Canada also has a bigger manufacturing sector than Australia.\par \par \uc2 There's "much less slack in the Australian economy than there is in the Canadian economy," Carney said.\par \par \uc2 He said pent-up demand and greater affordability has helped boost Canada's housing market in recent months.\par \par \uc2 He said the Bank's forecasts take into account "some strength" in housing and "we will continue to watch this closely".\par \par \uc2 He said the Bank is concerned about people who use low borrowing costs to get into the housing market who may face problems when rates eventually rise.\par \par \uc2 "We do have some concerns about it," he said. "Obviously consumer borrowing cannot grow faster than the economy forever."\par \par \uc2 He noted, however, that Canadian consumer balance sheets are starting from a much stronger position than in the U.S., and that debt-service ratios remain below historic averages.\par \par \uc2 He said the Bank is watching the growth in consumer credit.\par \par \uc2 "We expect prudence from lenders," and also consumers, he said.\par \par \uc2 Carney also said global current account imbalances are an important medium-term risk to the outlook for Canada and globally and there will have to be "further adjustments to structural monetary and exchange rate policies in some G20 countries". Talks on that have already begun at the G20 level, he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 613-237-0668; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com [ 10-22-09 1354ET ]\par \par \uc2 4213   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020091022e5am000pg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
