\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Afternoon: C$ Ends Down Modestly After More BOC Jawboning\b0\par\par\uc2 566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2009\par 21:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar ended moderately weaker Thursday, continuing a pattern of choppy and skittish dealings that have sapped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's previous momentum ever since Tuesday's dovish \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  policy statement.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.0483 at 3:41 p.m. EDT (1941 GMT), from C$1.0492 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT) and C$1.0444 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar spent most of Thursday chopping back and forth on both sides of the C$1.0500 figure, unable to take much support from stronger-than-expected Canadian August retail sales data or healthy gains for North American equity markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers said the Canadian dollar is still feeling some negative fallout from Tuesday's Bank statement, which offered a fairly dovish prognosis for interest rate policy into at least mid-2010 and doused previous speculation that the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be primed to begin raising rates earlier.\par \par \uc2 That message was reinforced with Thursday's release of the latest quarterly \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  monetary policy report, as well as by statements from \b \highlight22\uc2 central \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Mark Carney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Carney identified the strong Canadian dollar as the main risk to Canada's growth outlook in post-release comments to reporters, and said that market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other non-conventional means to tame the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and maintain the Bank's inflation targets are always options.\par \par \uc2 Carney's remarks weren't new in substance, but this latest salvo of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely helped keep a lid on the Canadian dollar's performance Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "I'd certainly say the comments by Carney added to the volatility today, but the underperformance of the Canadian dollar has been ongoing since Tuesday's policy statement," said Jack Spitz, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at National Bank in Toronto. "That's dragging on the broader market landscape for the Canadian dollar, and what the governor said today is what people already know."\par \par \uc2 Most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers and market participants would argue that while the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  has ample credibility and its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  potential is to be taken seriously, particularly if future Canadian dollar appreciation becomes disorderly and primarily speculative in nature, at least a somewhat stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely a foregone conclusion owing to the declining U.S. dollar's function as part of the ongoing resolution of global imbalances.\par \par \uc2 Economist Stewart Hall of HSBC Securities in Toronto said that in Thursday's policy report, "the Bank acknowledged that which is evident - Canadian dollar strength is a function of a weak U.S. dollar. On that basis, it is not a unique event for Canada alone."\par \par \uc2 "From that, one derives a sense of acceptance on behalf of the Bank that really, while all options remain open, there is little inclination to step in front of what amounts to an issue with U.S. dollars rather than something specific to the Canadian dollar," Hall added.\par \par \uc2 These are the exchange rates at 3:41 p.m. EDT (1941 GMT), 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    USD/CAD     1.0483      1.0492     1.0444 \par    EUR/CAD     1.5751      1.5734     1.5685 \par    CAD/JPY      87.16       86.92      87.00 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Paul Evans; Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2022; paulr.evans@dowjones.com [ 10-22-09 1545ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1158   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091022e5am000df\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
