\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Market doesn't buy tough \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  talk on C$\b0\par\par\uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2009\par 22:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Players question likelihood \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would work\par \par \uc2 * Commodities, broader US$ weakness behind C$ rise\par \par \uc2 * Skepticism makes move to parity more likely\par \par \uc2 By Jeffrey Hodgson\par \par \uc2 TORONTO, Oct 13 (Reuters) - Tough words about Canada's soaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have yet to convince markets that the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the rise, raising the odds that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will climb above the U.S. dollar for the first time since July last year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says a strong dollar could slow Canada's climb out of recession, and insists it has plenty of ammunition to slow the rise. The Canadian dollar <CAD=D3>, which hit a 14-month high of 97.42 U.S. cents on Tuesday, is up more than 27 percent since March.\par \par \uc2 But analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- selling the Canadian dollar on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to drive its price lower -- might not work, and few expect it.\par \par \uc2 "What are the chances that it's going to be effective? How much of this is Canadian dollar strength and how much of it is broad U.S. dollar weakness? The odds against successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seem high right now," said Marc Chandler, a strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "In the short run I don't see much that Canada can do."\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar, dubbed the loonie for the bird pictured on the one dollar coin, reached a modern-day high around US$1.10 in 2007.\par \par \uc2 That left exporters fretting about Canada's ability to compete and helped create Canada's first trade deficits in years. One industry group says a 1 percent rise in the Canadian dollar cuts manufacturing sales by about C$2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Yet analysts say higher prices for the commodities that Canada exports are largely behind the rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That makes it tougher for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to say the rise is driven by speculation -- a key criteria for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I don't think that it can be dismissed, but I don't think it's highly likely. I think the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  is very reticent to do it especially, given the overall dollar backdrop," said David Watt, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We do have the global economy starting to improve, commodity prices are doing well. And I think that the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  would struggle to get their communications strategy out in this situation."\par \par \uc2 NOT OUT OF BULLETS\par \par \uc2 Government and \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  officials have spent months trying to "jawbone" the Canadian dollar lower, warning repeatedly that it threatens the recovery and could cause inflation to undershoot the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two percent target.\par \par \uc2 Asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Governor Mark Carney\uc2 , a self-assured former \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  banker, said last month the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will do what is required" to hit that target. [ID:nN28514256]\par \par \uc2 He also stressed the bank is "not out of bullets" just because it has cut rates to a record low and promised to keep them there until at least the middle of next year, provided inflation remains under control.\par \par \uc2 The bank next sets rates Oct 20, followed two days later by a raft of new economic forecasts. So near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would clash with a news blackout ahead of rate decisions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a major Western \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the current crisis would not be unprecedented. The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  stunned markets in March when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Swiss franc, citing deflation risks. [ID:nLB459488]\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Canadian dollar since 1998, when it changed its policy to act only in "the most exceptional of circumstances".\par \par \uc2 These include disruptive, extreme short-term movements that could threaten the economy's long-term outlook.\par \par \uc2 "Their mandate is disorderly moves. Maybe they might consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just to smooth things out, but the move has been anything but disorderly and you just can't stand in the way of this freight train at the moment," said Steve Butler, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Scotia Capital.\par \par \uc2 Still, not all market players doubt the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's willingness to act. Based on its own criteria, the numbers would support the bank should it wish to act, said Neil Mellor, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Bank of New York Mellon in London.\par \par \uc2 "You have to put (the odds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at) a little higher perhaps than 50 percent. I think you have to, given what has been said. Otherwise it's empty rhetoric and the market knows that and it will carry on buying the (Canadian dollar)," he said. (With additional reporting by Frank Pingue and Louise Egan)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CANADA/DOLLAR (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|RBN|CANT|D|M|ABX|BNX    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091016e5ad001j8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw98\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1470\pichgoal600
