\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-POLL-\uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to keep interest rate pledge\b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2009\par 17:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rates seen steady at 0.25 pct for rest of 2009\par \par \uc2 * 4 dealers expect BoC rate hike in H1/2010\par \par \uc2 * Dealers see little chance of quantitative easing\par \par \uc2 By Frank Pingue\par \par \uc2 TORONTO, Sept 4 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  will stick to its conditional pledge to keep its benchmark interest rate at the current near-zero level through the second quarter of next year, most Canadian primary dealers said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Dealers also saw little chance that the \uc2 Bank of Canada\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par would engage in any form of quantitative easing -- printing\par money to buy market securities -- over the next 12 months. The\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 median expectation for that probability was 5 percent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The possibility of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, however, clung to the radar screen due to comments from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  last month that it has the tools at its disposal to deal with the appreciating Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . [ID:nN25220089]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A separate Reuters poll this week showed experts felt there was a 17.5 percent chance that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets to rein in the Canadian dollar's surge from its four-year low in March. [ID:nN02535283]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will announce its next rate decision on Thursday, though the result is largely a foregone conclusion because of its April pledge to leave rates steady until the end of the second quarter of 2010. [ID:nLL202349]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Eight of Canada's 12 primary dealers polled by Reuters predicted the slow pace of the economic recovery will keep the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  from lifting its key interest rate above the current 0.25 percent level until after the first half of 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But one dealer expected a rate hike as early as the first quarter of 2010. And two forecast the bank would hold off on raising rates till as late as the first quarter of 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The poll was conducted after data showed Canada's economy unexpectedly added jobs in August, a surprise that analysts said was another sign the economy was starting to recover after months of turmoil. [ID:nN04153956]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  COMPANY                      SEPT     OCT      DEC\par                               ACTION   ACTION   ACTION\par  BMO CAPITAL MARKETS          NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  CASGRAIN & CO LTD            NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 CIBC WORLD MARKETS INC\f28 \uc2 \f2 .      NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  DESJARDINS SECURITIES        NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  DEUTSCHE BANK SECURITIES LTD NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  HSBC SECURITIES              NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  LAURENTIAN BANK SECURITIES   NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  MERRILL LYNCH CANADA INC.    NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  NATIONAL BANK                NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  RBC CAPITAL MARKETS          NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  SCOTIA CAPITAL INC.          NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 TORONTO-DOMINION BANK\f28 \uc2 \f2         NO MOVE  NO MOVE  NO MOVE\par                               NEXT RATE HIKE\par                               EXPECTED\par  BMO CAPITAL MARKETS          Q3/2010\par  CASGRAIN & CO LTD