\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Ghost Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Haunts Canadian Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2009\par 13:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This article was originally published Thursday.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Don Curren \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The specter of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is haunting the Canadian dollar.\par \par \uc2 Like any other ghost, phantom \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  does its haunting through whispers, rumors and speculation rather than showing itself outright.\par \par \uc2 Analysts talk about it as well as write articles and reports on it. Some conjecture that \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is responsible for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Canadian system, could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quell strength in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it resumes its sharp push higher.\par \par \uc2 Among those who have mentioned \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are Canadian Finance Minister Jim Flaherty and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Mark Carney. Indeed, their statements of concern about the economic impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise and references to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a key reason the ghost continues to hover over the market.\par \par \uc2 But some sporadic references to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even from the top monetary officials in the country, don't mean that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely, even if the Canadian dollar shakes off its recent doldrums and resumes its push higher.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stated policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its recent history in the area suggests the probability of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains extremely low.\par \par \uc2 At one point, the bank did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently. Until September 1998, it had a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the market to resist significant upward or downward pressure in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . During the summer of 1998, as the Canadian dollar plummeted to all-time lows against the greenback, the bank was frequently in the market, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness.\par \par \uc2 That came to an abrupt halt that September, when the bank shifted to a radically different approach. It instituted a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a "discretionary" rather systematic basis, and of doing so "only in the most exceptional of circumstances," according to the section about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy on the bank's Web site.\par \par \uc2 What would qualify as the "most exceptional of circumstances"?\par \par \uc2 In 2007, the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  embarked on an unprecedented push higher, reaching its highest levels ever in the modern \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading era and breaking above parity with the U.S. dollar in the fall. It eventually pushed the U.S. dollar to C$0.9065 before it stopped its rampage and retreated.\par \par \uc2 That didn't qualify; the bank did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In fact, it has abstained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since it adopted its "discretionary" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the fall of '98, with the exception of participation in joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with four other key central banks in defense of a weakening euro in September 2000.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts have drawn this inference from the events of 2007: If the bank didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  then, it's extremely unlikely to do so now.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not only run counter to the bank's recent history and stated policy, but also would tend to contradict the foundations of its approach to monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The bank has one key policy objective: keeping inflation near its 2% target.\par \par \uc2 In order to set monetary policy freely and independently to achieve that objective, the bank needs to have a floating exchange rate. "Canada's flexible exchange rate permits us to pursue an independent monetary policy suited to the needs of our own economy and acts as a 'shock absorber'," the bank's Web site says. It also makes it clear the bank doesn't target any particular level for the exchange rate, believing it should be set by the market.\par \par \uc2 To \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to influence that rate would undermine the bank's hard-won reputation for consistency, credibility and transparency.\par \par \uc2 Does that mean \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be ruled out completely?\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 As Governor Carney has pointed out, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tool kit. It's always at least theoretically possible.\par \par \uc2 And that's one reason the ghost of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep on haunting the Canadian dollar, even if it remains only a specter.\par \par \uc2 -By Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020; don.curren@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Don Curren, a reporter based in Toronto, has covered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed-income markets for more than 10 years. He can be reached at 416-306-2020 or at don.curren@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 21, 2009 07:36 ET (11:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090821e58l00313\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
