\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  unlikely tool for Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 857 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2009\par 22:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Further jawboning likely course of action\par \par \uc2 * BoC last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1998\par \par \uc2 * Canadian dollar hovers near 10-mth high around 94 cents\par \par \uc2 By Ka Yan Ng\par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to take the highly unusual step of selling Canadian dollars in the market to brake the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, with policy makers limiting themselves to verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keen to clip the quick ascent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has hovered near a 10-month high this week, out of fear it could delay economic recovery. Finance Minister Jim Flaherty ramped up the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric on Tuesday by suggesting there were "steps" that policy makers could take to halt the appreciation.\par \par \uc2 But the bank lacks the tools needed to make a lasting impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value.\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be effective, it needs to be backed up by other policies, Bank of Canada Governor Mark Carney said himself last month. The bank retains "considerable flexibility" and will use it if necessary, he said.\par \par \uc2 But the options currently available are limited and would appear to contradict Carney's bravado.\par \par \uc2 "You need a follow-through to make it stick. I think that's the rub," said Eric Lascelles, chief rates and economics strategist at TD Securities.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Canada is in a bit of a tight corner. It has no wiggle room on interest rates, having chopped its benchmark rate to a floor of 0.25 percent with a conditional pledge to keep it there until mid-2010.\par \par \uc2 So the only other way for him to back up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be to engage in so-called quantitative and credit easing -- which could include creating money to buy bonds in the market. Carney has all but ruled out that option, saying it would take a big negative shock to the economy to make him change his mind. Analysts are not convinced the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation risk fits the bill.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar's rise isn't a broad financial shock. It's an external terms of trade shock that in part reflects the fundamentals," said Derek Holt, an economist at Scotia Capital. "You don't want to prescribe the wrong policy fix."\par \par \uc2 As an institution, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is extremely wary of meddling with the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate after unsuccessful bids to do so in the 1990s. It has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998, the year it changed its policy to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in "the most exceptional of circumstances."\par \par \uc2 They did not step in during its runup toward parity in late 2007 or when it stayed near par until July 2008.\par \par \uc2 And Carney would risk a backlash from his G20 colleagues after Canada pledged along with other major world economies earlier this year not to engage in competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Canadian exporters, the backbone of the economy, have been burdened by a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they generate most of their sales revenue in U.S. dollars. Similar appreciation in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of export-based economies in other corners of the globe have sparked \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The "some steps" talk by Flaherty renewed some nervousness in the market as the Canadian dollar pulled back from a 10-month high, above 94 U.S. cents, and briefly extended losses Wednesday in contrast to other rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more than 20 percent above March's four-year low and within striking distance of topping the U.S. dollar for the first time in a year.\par \par \uc2 But Carney and Flaherty could lose credibility if their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk continues without having the desired effect.\par \par \uc2 "As time passes the comments will be forgotten unless that is backed up action and I don't think it will be," said Adam Cole, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at RBC Capital Markets in London.\par \par \uc2 Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take the shine off the Canadian dollar temporarily, in a backdrop that features global hopes of economic recovery. Rising commodity prices should also keep it from falling too far from current levels above 90 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 A monthly Reuters poll showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will steadily back off the 10-month high reached earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 What could happen is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  extends its promise to keep rates unchanged beyond the end of second quarter target.\par \par \uc2 "To me the most effective route is you just reinforce the message that rates are on hold for long, long time, and maybe longer than the current conditional statement," said Holt.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, market players remain vigilant for any sign that officials are becoming more concerned about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think at this point, the Bank of Canada probably is enjoying the position it is in -- which is not having to actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but perhaps deriving some small benefit from the speculation so far as some investors may be reluctant to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too far in the event that it did prompt a response," said Lascelles.\par \par \uc2 "A bit of jawboning never hurt anyone."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ka Yan Ng; Editing by Frank McGurty)\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-\b \highlight22\uc2 CANADA\b0 \highlight\uc2 /\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090808e585000d7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
