\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-\uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  move highlights credit market thaw\b0\par\par\uc2 1,088 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2009\par 21:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Credit market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  trimmed as funding costs fall\par \par \uc2 * Thaw seen supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth outlook, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Analysts confident funding cost drop likely to hold\par \par \uc2 By Frank Pingue and Jeffrey Hodgson\par \par \uc2 TORONTO, July 21 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's decision on Tuesday to scale back on credit market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows lending conditions are moving closer to pre-crisis levels, which should support markets and the still struggling economy.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision reflects growing confidence that the global credit crunch that preceded the financial meltdown is unlikely to resume any time soon.\par \par \uc2 "The worst is definitely behind us," said Michael Gregory, senior economist at BMO Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 "What it does mean is that shorter-term borrowing costs for consumers and businesses, which are above and beyond what banks charge one another, can remain relatively low, and as low as they possibly can be."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  outlined the changes to its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its interest rate announcement on Tuesday, in which it held its key rate at a record low of 0.25 percent as expected and gave a rosier economic forecast. [CA/INT]\par \par \uc2 It said that because conditions in short-term lending markets have improved, it would cut the minimum amounts auctioned through its term purchase and resale agreements and related facilities. In these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  the bank temporarily buys securities in an effort to improve liquidity. (The schedule can be found at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bankofcanada.ca/en/notices_fmd/2009/notice_270709.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.bankofcanada.ca/en/notices_fmd/2009/notice_270709.pdf}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Scotia Capital economist Derek Holt said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's scaling back of the program was "more concrete than expected" and reflected a dramatic improved in funding conditions.\par \par \uc2 "In a lot of respects, the markets had already scaled back on these initiatives. And so this is really just the bank's acknowledgment that the private industry players have signaled that they don't need them as aggressively any more," he said.\par \par \uc2 LIQUIDITY AUCTION SNUBBED\par \par \uc2 Signs that the need for such programs is declining emerged last week when the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  managed to buy just C$2.25 billion ($2 billion) worth of securities in a C$3 billion PRA \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first time there had not been full demand since the facility was launched. [ID:nN13207877]\par \par \uc2 Behind the drop in demand is a sharp fall in the rates banks charge to lend among themselves. These had spiked last year as firms such as \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  failed and panic set in about which institution would be next.\par \par \uc2 In Canada, the three-month Canadian dealer offered rate <CA3MBAFIX=><CDOR>, similar to the key Libor (London interbank offered rate) used in other markets, fell from around 3.73 percent at the start of October to just 0.436 percent on Tuesday, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 The thaw in money markets has been a global trend, with the benchmark three-month rates on U.S. dollar, euro and sterling funds that banks lend among themselves hitting record lows this month.\par \par \uc2 "There were concerns that at the height of the crisis period you simply couldn't get financing for some debt, regardless of the rate. It was a question of simply being able to get something done," said Mark Chandler, fixed income strategist \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think that with a great amount of confidence you can say we won't revisit the lows."\par \par \uc2 NOT BACK TO NORMAL\par \par \uc2 The Toronto-based strategist said a factor underpinning the recovery is the commitment by the Group of 20 nations to prevent systemically important institutions from failing.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the changes in seemingly arcane interbank lending rates have a very real effect on the economy, influencing everything from businesses raising short term funds to finance inventory, to capital spending projects.\par \par \uc2 BMO's Gregory said the thawing of Canadian short-term lending markets should ultimately help support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <CAD=><CAD=D3> because it helps reduce the need for the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to keep policy accommodative.\par \par \uc2 But he warned that policymakers and investors shouldn't be complacent as the credit market fallout from the crisis is still weighing on the financial system and the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 "It is still hard for some entities to raise money in the markets and fund their lending activities ... so things aren't back to normal," Gregory said.\par \par \uc2 "But I think Canada, in terms of functioning in the financial system, is functioning much better than virtually all the other competing markets in the G7."\par \par \uc2 ($1=$1.11 Canadian) (Editing by Rob Wilson) [20090721 194844 GMT]\par \par \uc2 ============================================================== FOR CANADIAN MARKETS NEWS, CLICK ON CODES IN BRACKETS: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian dollar and bonds report....[CAD/][CA/]\par TSX market report..........[.TO]\par Headlines f