\par\par \b \uc2 CANADA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  DEBT-C$ ends above 90 US cents as sentiment improves\b0\par\par\uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2009\par 15:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Climbs as high as C$1.1019 to the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 * Commodity rally, risk appetite drive gains\par \par \uc2 * Bond prices turn around, end mostly higher\par \par \uc2 (Updates to session close)\par \par \uc2 By Frank Pingue\par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar raced to its highest level against the greenback in over five weeks Monday, thanks to an improved appetite for risk and stronger commodity prices, but it gave back a slice of its early gains ahead of Tuesday's \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate decision.\par \par \uc2 The rally came alongside advances in global equities, as strong U.S. corporate earnings and news that lender \uc2 CIT Group Inc\uc2  was thrown a financial lifeline to avoid bankruptcy helped reinforce optimism for a global recovery.\par \par \uc2 That all helped to send the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as high as C$1.1019 to the U.S. dollar, or 90.75 U.S. cents, which marked its highest level since June 12. The move followed last week's 4.4 percent rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Problems with the economy are still prevalent but people are driving stocks upward with speculation that the economy is in the recovery stage," said Tyson Wright, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Custom House, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  services firm in British Columbia. "Therefore risk assets are in demand and the Canadian dollar is gaining on the back of that sentiment."\par \par \uc2 By the end of the session the Canadian dollar backed down to C$1.1068 to the U.S. dollar, or 90.35 U.S. cents, but was still comfortably above Friday's close of C$1.1161 to the U.S. dollar, or 89.60 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 Higher prices for oil and commodities, key Canadian exports, also helped lure traders to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar's rise came ahead of the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's interest rate announcement Tuesday, when it is expected to stick to its conditional pledge to keep rates at the current near-zero level.\par \par \uc2 Plenty of eyes will likely be on the bank's accompanying statement to see if it offers an updated view on the Canadian dollar. In its June statement, the bank said a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could offset positive factors such as improved financial conditions and commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , the recent strength of the Canadian dollar is on the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's radar screen, but it would still need to rise much further to convince the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "The (\uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is likely to continue to rely on rhetoric to cap (Canadian dollar) rallies," David Watt, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 "However, if CAD maintains its momentum, short of signs of a stunning rebound in the global economy, the risk of a stronger response will increase exponentially as USD/CAD head well below C$1.10."\par \par \uc2 BONDS PRICES REBOUND\par \par \uc2 Domestic bond prices reversed an early selloff to close the session mostly higher as potential for bond-friendly remarks from central bankers convinced dealers to square positions.\par \par \uc2 In addition to Tuesday's \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate announcement, dealers are getting set for U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's semi-annual testimony to Congress.\par \par \uc2 Positioning ahead of those events helped reverse an early selloff in bonds, which came on news that \uc2 CIT Group\uc2  may have reached a deal that could allow it to avoid bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 That news helped initially lure investors out of government debt and and into stocks, even pushing the S&P/TSX composite index briefly to its highest level since June 15.\par \par \uc2 "It seemed to be risk appetites were were building again this morning but things turned around a little bit here despite the fact that data today ... was actually a little bit better than expected," said Michael Gregory, senior economist at BMO Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 "I suspect that what we saw here was just simply a little bit of position squaring perhaps ahead of tomorrow's policy pronouncements on both sides of the border."\par \par \uc2 Canadian data showed wholesale trade dropped in May for the eighth consecutive month to its lowest level since 2005, while a separate report showed foreigners scooped up a record C$19.38 billion worth of Canadian bonds in May.\par \par \uc2 The two-year Canada bond ended down 1 Canadian cent at C$100.07 to yield 1.214 percent, while the 10-year bond rose 53 Canadian cents to C$102.65 to yield 3.430 percent.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year bond rose C$1.15 to C$117.60 to yield 3.952 percent. The 30-year U.S. Treasury bond yielded 4.510 percent.\par \par \uc2 Canadian bonds outperformed their U.S. counterparts across the long end of the curve. The Canadian 30-year bond was about 56 basis points below the U.S. 30-year yield, compared with 53 basis points on Friday. (Editing by Rob Wilson)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CANADA/DOLLAR-BONDS (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|Table|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090723e57k0016l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
