\par\par \b \uc2 BOC WATCH: Survey Sees Stand-Pat Stance At Thu Policy Call\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Evans   \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2009\par 17:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Given the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's standing commitment to hold its key policy interest rate at an ultra-low 0.25% for the next year, one of the only mysteries surrounding Thursday's scheduled policy decision is whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is any closer to adopting quantitative easing or other unorthodox policy measures.\par \par \uc2 The unanimous consensus of ten analysts at Canadian primary securities dealers surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires is a resounding no on that question.\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  issued its last rate decision and Monetary Policy Report in late April, it did so with a conditional promise to hold its key rate at 0.25% until the end of 2010's second quarter, contingent on the outlook for inflation.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlined a range of possible credit and quantitative easing tools, stressing that they would only be deployed if the present recession deepens.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  said then that in its judgement, its conditional low-rate commitment in conjunction with the cumulative 425 basis points of rate easing since December 2007 will be sufficient to revive the Canadian economy without the deployment of any extraordinary policy measures.\par \par \uc2 Developments since then, according to the overwhelming consensus view, have validated this view, meaning it would be a shock of thunderous proportions should the Bank announce any unorthodox policy measures Thursday.\par \par \uc2 To begin with, domestic and international financial conditions have eased markedly since the beginning of the year, with both short-term bank funding costs and bellwether corporate yields spreads having subsided well off the peaks reached in late 2008 and early 2009, according to \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The domestic economy has also shown signs that its rate of deterioration is slowing, as Canada's first quarter real gross domestic product topped the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's own pessimistic projection of a 7.3% decline by contracting only 5.4% instead.\par \par \uc2 "If the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  wasn't prepared to do anything in the first quarter of the year, now that we're looking at market conditions that have significantly improved since then, I'm not sure what the justification could be for introducing these types of programs now," said economist Stewart Hall of HSBC Securities Canada in downplaying the likelihood of any major surprises in Thursday's statement.\par \par \uc2 That's not to say that Thursday's communique isn't at risk of at least some departures from the status quo.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar has rocketed up some 14% in value since the beginning of April, trading well above the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's assumed trading range and representing an effective tightening of monetary conditions.\par \par \uc2 Accordingly, some see a possibility, but not an outright likelihood, that the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  might begin alluding to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in terms of a new risk to the outlook Thursday, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's standing reluctance for explicit commentary on the Canadian dollar.\par \par \uc2 "I think they'll hold off and not send any kind of strong signal of displeasure," said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sacha Tihanyi of Scotia Capital, considering that the Canadian dollar's rise has come as part of a concerted global move against the U.S. dollar. "I certainly don't think they'll come anywhere close to threatening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - that's just not the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's way."\par \par \uc2 The following institutions are united in predicting no change to the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's 0.25% key policy rate, as well as the absence of any new, unorthodox policy measures Thursday. Most institutions also see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's conditional 0.25% rate commitment as lasting for the duration of the Bank's projected June 2010 time horizon.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    BMO CAPITAL MARKETS \par    CIBC WORLD MARKETS \par    DESJARDINS GROUP \par    DEUTSCHE BANK \par    HSBC \par    LAURENTIAN BANK SECURITIES \par    NATIONAL BANK OF CANADA \par    RBC FINANCIAL GROUP \par    SCOTIA CAPITAL \par    TD BANK FINANCIAL GROUP \par  \par    (Paul Evans, a reporter based in Toronto, has covered the Canadian economy, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and fixed-income markets for Dow Jones Newswires for over 10 years. He can be reached at 416-306-2022 or at paulr.evans@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 03-06-09 1548GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090603e563000oi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
