\par\par \b \uc2 Opening statement by Mr. Henri-Paul Rousseau, President and Chief Executive Officer of the  \uc2 Caisse de d\u233\'00\'E9p\u244\'00\'F4t et placement du Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec\uc2 , on the occasion of the review of the commercial paper crisis by the Committee on Public Finance of the National Assembly of Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec   \b0\par\par\uc2 4,190 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2007\par 04:53\par \uc2 Canada NewsWire\par \uc2 CNNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9  2007  Canada NewsWire Ltd. All rights reserved.      \par \par \uc2 \f2 QU\u201\'00\'C9BEC CITY, Nov. 28 /CNW/ -- \par     <<\par                            Check against delivery\par \par                               November 28, 2007\par \par     Opening statement by Mr. Henri-Paul Rousseau, President and Chief\par     Executive Officer of the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse de d\u233\'00\'E9p\u244\'00\'F4t et placement du Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec\f28 \uc2 \f2 , on the\par     occasion of the review of the commercial paper crisis by the Committee on\par     Public Finance of the National Assembly of Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec\par     >>\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 QU\u201\'00\'C9BEC CITY, Nov. 28 /CNW Telbec/ -   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     <<\par     -------------------------------------------------------------------------\par \par     Mr. President,\par     Madam Minister,\par     Members of the National Assembly,\par \par     Thank you for your invitation.\par \par     Before going any further, there are a number of things that I should like\par to state quite clearly.\par \par     The Caisse is in excellent financial shape.\par \par     The same is true of its depositors.\par \par     Over the past four years, the Caisse has made a great deal of money, with\par returns of 15.2%, 12.2%, 14.7% and 14.6%.\par \par     Since December 31, 2002, depositors' net assets have increased by\par $65.8\u160\'00\'A0billion, going from $77.7 billion to $143.5 billion at the end of 2006.\par Our returns account for 84%, or $55.3 billion, of that growth. Simply put,\par this is a return of $13.8 billion a year.\par \par     Our depositors have received very substantial amounts of money.\par \par     Those were very good years. The Caisse outperformed the market and\par outperformed its Canadian peers.\par \par     It is important to make as much money as possible in good years, because\par in the world of finance, some years are good and others are not so good.\par \par     Moreover, each year for the past four years, I have said and written that\par returns like the ones we were earning were not sustainable over the long term.\par \par     The depositors' long-term target return is 7%, and the average return for\par the past four years is 14.15%.\par \par     The financial health of the Caisse and of its depositors is therefore\par good, even very good. Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec's contributors and retirees do not have to be\par concerned.\par \par     The current problems on Canada's commercial paper market are not going to\par change that.\par \par     There are at least four reasons why I can make that statement:\par \par     - First, the assets underlying the ABCP held by the Caisse are of very\par       good quality; that has been confirmed by the work of the Pan Canadian\par       Committee overseeing the restructuring of this market;\par \par     - Second, the Caisse's exposure to the well-publicised U.S. subprime risk\par       is limited, and the eventual provisions regarding these investments\par       will not change the depositors' financial equilibrium;\par \par     - Third, there is no liquidity crisis at the Caisse. We have classified\par       all our holdings of third-party ABCP as medium-term investments. We\par       have re-established the necessary liquidity to meet our depositors'\par       needs and to continue making investments. The Caisse therefore has no\par       need to liquidate its investments in ABCP.\par \par     - Fourth, the Caisse is a long-term investor and fully has the means to\par       wait until it investments in ABCP produce the expected return. That is\par       not true for all the investors, but it is for the Caisse.\par \par     Finally, I would like to point out that commercial paper is a quality\par financial product. It is true that Canadian ABCP includes subprime mortgages,\par but in a very limited proportion. We now know that Canadian ABCP appears to\par correspond more or less to what it was deemed to be, namely assets of very\par good quality.\par \par     We will see, however, that Canada has a specific variation of this global\par crisis. The mechanisms that were supposed to maintain the market did not work,\par essentially because the international banks that were supposed to provide\par liquidity lines in such a situation did not do so.\par \par     From the outset, the Caisse assumed leadership in Canada by bringing\par together investors to put in place a solution to the problem. The\par restructuring of the market by the Pan Canadian Committee that is overseeing\par this project is well on its way and will ensure an orderly resolution of what\par was a crisis and is now being put into proper perspective.\par \par     That is the essence of my comments.\par \par     Now let's take a look at some of the details.\par \par     First, I am compelled to state clearly and unequivocally that at no time\par during my tenure as head of the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse de d\u233\'00\'E9p\u244\'00\'F4t et placement du Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec\f28 \uc2 \f2  have I\par encountered any direct or indirect interference from anyone who has held the\par position of Premier or Minister of Finance or any other political or\par administrative position in the government, whether in this government or the\par previous government.\par \par     The Board of Directors and senior managers are legally responsible for the\par Caisse's management and results, and we are always surprised when this\par responsibility is attributed to politicians who are not and must not be\par involved in these decisions.\par \par     As the most senior officer, I am fully accountable for the Caisse's\par results and the quality of its management, and I assume my responsibilities.\par \par     You will therefore understand that I was impatient to meet with you in\par order to respond to your questions and concerns.\par \par     In addition to ABCP, I know that economic development is of interest to\par you and I can also answer your questions on that matter.\par \par     In the normal course of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , under its incorporating Act, the\par Caisse prepares audited financial statements as at December 31 of each year\par and makes a full accounting in its annual report, made public in the spring.\par The Caisse's financial statements are audited by the Auditor General of\par Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec.\par \par     Under the Act and the agreements with its depositors, Caisse senior\par managers also report regularly to the Board of Directors and to the\par depositors, on both its results and its management.\par \par     But you will say that the commercial paper crisis is not part of the\par normal course of things.\par \par     So why has the Caisse not disclosed the figures pertaining to its\par investment in third-party ABCP?\par \par     If we have not done so until now, it is because we decided, with the\par agreement of the Board of Directors, that it was not in the depositors'\par interests to do so.\par \par     Here is the explanation.\par \par     Canada's commercial paper crisis does not stem above all from the problem\par of subprime mortgages in the United States, even though this problem was one\par cause of the crisis.\par \par     We now know that subprimes represent only a small percentage of the total\par ABCP that is being restructured, and that 97% of this paper consists of\par high-quality assets, as recently established by the Crawford Committee as well\par as by DBRS.\par \par     The Canadian crisis is therefore not a credit crisis but a made-in-Canada\par liquidity crisis, caused essentially by the large international banks, which\par realized that, as a result of a loophole in Canadian regulation, they possibly\par could not be legally obliged to honour their liquidity undertakings in Canada\par and thus could use their liquid assets elsewhere in the world.\par \par     The result was a complete standstill for transactions involving this paper\par and the crisis we know. After making many telephone calls in Canada and\par outside the country and realizing that the lack of liquidity could have\par repercussions not only for the investors concerned but also for the Canadian\par market as a whole, the Caisse quickly took the initiative of bringing together\par other investors to find a solution.\par \par     That is how we ended up, August 15, 2007, in the middle of the night, in a\par meeting room at the Caisse, with other Canadian investors, namely the federal\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Public Sector Pension Investment Board\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PSP), the Desjardins Group, National\par Bank, CIBC, Scotiabank and the rating agency DBRS, negotiating with the large\par international banks.\par \par     These meetings gave rise to what is now referred to as the Montr\u233\'00\'E9al\par Accord. Although CIBC and Scotiabank refused to sign on, the agreement binds\par the international banks involved in Canadian ABCP. The signatory banks are\par ABN\u160\'00\'A0AMRO, Barclay's Capital,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 ,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  and  \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS.\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par     Since September 6, 2007, the agreement has been overseen by a Pan Canadian\par Committee chaired by Purdy Crawford. In addition to the original members, a\par number of other Canadian investors have joined the Committee. They are Alberta\par Treasury Board Financial, Canada Post,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Union Central of British\par Columbia\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Credit Union Central Alberta, Credit Union of Ontario, Credit Union\par of Saskatchewan, Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Union Central\par of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 ,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canaccord Capital Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2 ,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 NAV CANADA\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Northwater Capital\par Management, University of Alberta and  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magna International.\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par     The agreement was initially concluded until October 14, 2007, but has\par since been extended until December 14, 2007.\par \par     Between August 15 and September 6, the objective of all the parties was to\par progress from crisis management to control over the situation.\par \par     The initial period was complex and difficult, because we had to gather the\par investors around the table and ensure that the interests of the various\par stakeholders converged.\par \par     It meant increasing the percentage of support for the Montr\u233\'00\'E9al Accord by\par ensuring that the Canadian banks, which had not signed the agreement,\par contacted their clients and persuaded them to join the agreement. It also\par meant co-ordinating numerous stakeholders on a completely private basis,\par including investors, distributors, trustees and limited partners. Lastly, it\par meant obtaining from the large international banks the information needed for\par an orderly negotiation.\par \par     And Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec's Minister of Finance was instrumental in helping me and the\par Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec signatories to the agreement accomplish the three things I just listed.\par \par     Her initiatives with respect to the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the federal\par Department of Finance were very important in enabling us to stabilize the\par situation. She did so tactfully because she had to respect the confidential\par and strategic nature of the information I provided her with, while respecting\par the Caisse's independence in accordance with the applicable rules of\par governance and while playing her role as the ultimate fiduciary. Her\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the Governor of the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the federal Deputy\par Minister of Finance contributed to the successful creation of the Crawford\par Committee and our obtaining the support of 84% of Canada's third-party ABCP\par investors.\par \par     You should know that, from the outset of the negotiation, we planned to\par make public the amounts invested by the Caisse in third-party ABCP.\par \par     We rapidly saw, however, that our counterparts at the large international\par banks were not prepared to disclose the information required to negotiate\par solutions.\par \par     Given the extreme difficulty of these talks on access to information, we\par decided, like the other members of the Committee, that it would not be in the\par interests of the investors to disclose our own information until the\par negotiations on access to information were complete and all the parameters\par were known. In other words, there was no question of showing our hand\par prematurely. That would have weakened the position of the Committee, which had\par to speak with a single voice during the negotiation, which was crucial to the\par success of the restructuring.\par \par     Moreover, the Committee adopted a very strict framework for the control\par and confidentiality of information. Each investor and each trust disclosed its\par information on a confidential basis to Ernst & Young, which acted as an\par information intermediary between the various parties. Ernst & Young in turn\par provided the information in aggregate form to  \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the financial\par adviser selected by the Committee.\par \par     To give you an idea of the complexity of the situation, the negotiations\par with the large international banks regarding access to information took two\par months, lasting from August 15 to mid-October, 2007.\par \par     This long period was due to the fact that, under the Montr\u233\'00\'E9al Accord, the\par signatories retained their right to take legal action against the\par international and Canadian banks. Yet, to negotiate an orderly solution, we\par had to obtain strategic information from the banks that had signed the\par agreement, although this information might be useful in an eventual lawsuit.\par To say that it was a very complicated situation is an understatement.\par \par     Once these steps were completed, the quality of the information obtained\par from the banks was analyzed by the adviser to the Crawford Committee,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P.\par Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which confirmed its validity at the end of October.\par \par     When the Board of Directors met on October 25, 2007, it reiterated the\par relevance of respecting the Caisse's annual reporting framework and the\par agreement with the Crawford Committee.\par \par     With the agreement of the Board, however, I informed the Minister of\par Finance that the Chairman and I were prepared meet with the members of the\par Committee on Public Finance if they deemed it advisable.\par \par     The Minister suggested such an invitation to the National Assembly on\par October 30. On Thursday, November 22, we received your invitation to today's\par meeting.\par \par     In short, from August 15 to late October, the Caisse could not contemplate\par disclosure without running the risk of jeopardizing the negotiation and\par protection of the holdings of the depositors and the Crawford Committee\par members.\par \par     Of the 17 members of the Committee, only four have officially disclosed\par their position in the ABCP market, namely National Bank, the Desjardins Group,\par Alberta Treasury Board Financial and  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magna International.\f28 \uc2 \f2  These four members\par were obliged to do so because of various obligations applying specifically to\par them.\par \par     With issues access concerning to and analysis of the information resolved,\par Caisse senior management and the Board have decided to make public today the\par level of our investments in third-party ABCP and the level of our exposure to\par U.S. subprimes. The members of the Crawford Committee have been informed of\par this decision and did not object.\par \par     Now let's look at the figures.\par \par     At the end of 2006, the Caisse had total assets of $207.9 billion. This\par amount consisted of $143.5 billion of depositors' net assets and $64.4 billion\par of assets financed by borrowing.\par \par     As at December 31, 2006, the total assets were diversified among the\par following investment categories: bonds, publicly traded equities, private\par equity, real estate holdings, real estate debt and investment funds. The\par assets also included short-term investments amounting to $33.6 billion, or 16%\par of total assets. These short-term investments constitute the Caisse's liquid\par assets, in other words investments deemed to offer a high degree of liquidity\par and a low level of risk.\par \par     The Caisse maintains liquid assets for three main reasons:\par \par     First, to meet the requirements of the depositors, which, in their\par investment policies, allocate a portion of their funds to this investment\par class;\par \par     Second, being liquid means we can rapidly take advantage of business\par opportunities to enhance depositors' returns;\par \par     Finally, the sale of investments, the receipt of dividends and interest,\par and the proceeds of financial engineering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increase the Caisse's\par liquid assets.\par \par     The Caisse manages all these liquid assets centrally, regardless of the\par specialized portfolios they belong to.\par \par     Since the start of the crisis, third-party ABCP has not been included in\par the Caisse's liquid assets.\par \par     Despite this change, the Caisse was able to re-establish a solid liquidity\par position.  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued a press release on September 28 confirming\par our AAA rating and strong liquidity position. The two other rating agencies\par were also informed of the situation and maintained their AAA ratings.\par \par     What is even more important, the Caisse continues to carry out its\par business plan and it has the necessary liquid assets take advantage of all\par investment opportunities.\par \par     The Caisse has a total of $13.2 billion invested in third-party ABCP.\par \par     This amount may seem high. Moreover, it is correct to say that, in dollar\par terms, the Caisse is the investor that holds the most third-party ABCP in\par Canada. But as a percentage of its equity, if we compare it to those who have\par disclosed their positions to date, the Caisse is the least exposed. The Caisse\par was therefore not the only Canadian investor with a strategy that assigned an\par important role to third-party ABCP.\par \par     Now let's talk about subprimes.\par \par     Of the $13.2 billion, the Caisse's exposure to subprimes is slightly less\par than $1 billion.\par \par     But let me make this clear: not all of the one billion dollars of exposure\par to subprimes will eventually be subject to a provision.\par \par     Currently, the provisions taken by the large international banks represent\par from 30% to 50% of their subprime exposure.\par \par     Assuming a provision as high as 50%, for the Caisse that would mean a\par provision of $500 million. Such a provision would have an impact of 30 basis\par points on the Caisse's return, or 0.3%, which is not likely to affect the\par financial equilibrium of the Caisse and its depositors.\par \par     To cite another point of comparison,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimated in a recent\par study that the provisions of the large international banks for their U.S.\par subprime exposure were equivalent to 8% of their equity. For the Caisse, as I\par have stated, this ratio appears to be 0.3% of net assets as at December 31,\par 2006.\par \par     The remainder, namely $12.2 billion, consists of assets of very good\par quality, as confirmed by  \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the adviser to the Crawford Committee.\par \par     Now let's look at another important matter.\par \par     As you know, a few publicly traded companies recently had to state the\par fair value of the third-party ABCP they hold. It is important to note that\par these companies had no choice but to do so now, in light of their obligation\par to publicly disclose their financial results and generally accepted accounting\par principles in Canada.\par \par     The timing of this valuation could not have been more poorly chosen,\par however, because the market is experiencing a liquidity crisis and the\par Crawford Committee has not yet finished its work.\par \par     We must draw a clear distinction between a valuation that establishes a\par liquidation value rather than an ultimate recovery value. It is necessarily\par the recovery value that must apply in the case of a solid investor prepared to\par wait for repayment of the underlying debt when nothing indicates that such\par repayment is in jeopardy. The quality of the assets is the essential\par criterion.\par \par     For example, more than a third of the Caisse's assets are not liquid, such\par as private equity and real estate holdings. But these assets are not recorded\par at their liquidation value.\par \par     Thus far, investors holding up to $10 million of third-party ABCP have\par taken writedowns ranging from 0% to 40%. Those holding from $10 million to\par $100 million have taken provisions ranging from 0% to 15.4%. Those holding\par $100 million to $1 billion have taken writedowns ranging from 0% to 19.3%. The\par decrease in value for the three investors with direct exposure exceeding\par $1\u160\'00\'A0billion is 6.5%, 8.4% and 25%.\par \par     There are several reasons for these differences:\par \par     - First, there is the degree of subprime exposure;\par     - Second, there is the fact that the restructuring of the market is not\par       complete;\par     - Third, the write-downs taken by certain institutions may be greater\par       because they simultaneously played several roles on the ABCP market,\par       acting as trustees, distributors and advisers as well as suppliers of\par       assets and liquidity. That is not true of the Caisse;\par     - And, fourth, there is diversification. Unlike the Caisse, certain small\par       investors did not have the same degree of diversification among the\par       various trusts, which also explains the differences observed.\par \par     The Caisse will therefore determine the fair value of its third-party ABCP\par as at December 31, 2007, according to the context and the circumstances\par prevailing at that time. Now that we know what our subprime exposure is,\par what's left is to do a valuation of the other assets as at December 31, 2007.\par We will do so rigorously, but since the quality of the assets has been\par demonstrated and the Caisse is a patient investor, none of the scenarios\par contemplated is likely to change the financial equilibrium of the depositors,\par given, among other factors, the amounts earned in the past four years.\par \par     As he does each year, the Auditor General of Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec has already filed his\par audit plan for 2007, and it includes a review of the ABCP valuation. The\par Caisse's overall audited results for 2007 will be made public in February\par 2008.\par \par     At this point, I should like to state that I have agreed with the main\par depositors that I would inform them of, and that I would make public today,\par the allocation of the $13.2 billion of third-party ABCP to their respective\par accounts. These amounts are generally speaking in close proportion to their\par relative weight in the net assets of $143.5 billion.\par \par     The total $13.2 billion of ABCP held in the accounts of the various\par depositors breaks down as follows:\par \par     - RREGOP        $4.0 billion\par     - RRQ           $2.8 billion\par     - FARR          $2.7 billion\par     - CCQ           $854 million\par     - CSS           $779 million\par     - RRPE          $671 million\par     - SAAQ          $634 million\par     - Other         $650 million\par \par     In passing, I should like to thank the depositors for their continuing\par support for the Caisse in recent months, their understanding and their\par patience. I know, because I have spoken with the representatives of many of\par them, that they understand the situation and that they support the Caisse's\par actions in this matter.\par \par     You may ask why the Caisse invested in this financial instrument when\par other Canadian investors did not.\par \par     We must bear in mind that the Canadian ABCP market has two components.\par That of the Canadian banks is estimated at $83 billion and that of third\par parties is estimated at $33 billion.\par \par     The Caisse, like many other sophisticated investors in Canada, believed\par that a liquidity crisis on a market of more than $116 billion was highly\par unlikely for three reasons:\par \par     - First, a standstill on the Canadian ABCP market as a result of\par       contamination by U.S. subprimes, while not impossible, was unlikely;\par \par     - Second, international and Canadian banks were expected to provide the\par       necessary liquidity to the market in such a situation, which they did\par       not do as a result of a certain interpretation of Canadian regulation;\par       that also appeared to be unlikely;\par \par     - Third, we were convinced that if the one component of the market\par       experienced a liquidity crisis, the other would also be affected and\par       that, since the market represented $116 billion, the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par       would provide liquidity not only to the Canadian banks but also to the\par       entire market. We now know that the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  assisted the bank\par       component of the market but could not assist the third-party market.\par \par     Moreover, the Deputy Governor of the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Pierre Duguay, stated\par in an address he gave in Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec City on November 20, and I quote: "This raises\par the question of whether some market failures might be better dealt with if the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  established mechanisms that would provide liquidity at terms\par longer than overnight, collateralized with a possibly wider range of\par securities."\par \par     Beyond the matter of ABCP, I believe it is important to say a few words\par about the year in progress.\par \par     The 2007 returns earned by Canadian pension funds will be strongly\par influenced by the four following factors: the appreciation of the Canadian\par dollar against the world's main \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the impact of the credit crisis\par caused by subprime loans in the United States, the extreme volatility of the\par financial markets and, lastly, the liquidity crisis affecting Canada's\par non-bank ABCP.\par \par     For example, figures available on the performance of pension funds in the\par third quarter of 2007 showed that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -hedging strategies have had a\par determining impact during the year in progress. In this respect, the Caisse's\par hedging strategy alone will have a positive impact of several billion dollars\par on its 2007 results.\par \par     We will obviously have to wait until year-end to measure precisely the\par impact of the markets and the Caisse's investment decisions in 2007 but I\par remain confident about this matter.\par \par                                     (xxx)\par \par     In conclusion, I would like to address another matter raised by the\par situation, namely: "Does the Caisse assume too much risk in its fund\par management?"\par \par     The answer to this question is most definitely no. In fact, in recent\par years the Caisse has assumed less risk and has obtained better results.\par \par     The Caisse's objective is to produce value added, namely a performance\par superior to that of the markets alone. The Caisse's basic value-added target\par is 90 basis points. Since 1986, or for 20 years, the only consecutive\par three-year period in which this value-added target was exceeded and when the\par Caisse also ranked in the first quartile of large Canadian pension funds was\par from 2004 to 2006. That is shown in table 1 appended to the text of this\par statement. We achieved this historic performance while reducing the level of\par risk in relation to our peers, as shown by the figure in appendix 2.\par \par     It would therefore be wrong to state that the Caisse assumes too much\par risk. On the contrary, it succeeds, while assuming less risk, in producing\par better returns than its Canadian peers.\par \par     Thank you for your attention.\par \par \par     To consult the appendices 1 and 2, please click on the following link:\par       \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://files.newswire.ca/658/cdpva2.doc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://files.newswire.ca/658/cdpva2.doc  }}}\uc2 \f2 \par     >>\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Gilles des Roberts, (514) 847-2175   \par \par \uc2 200711282253CANADANWCANADAPR_C5590.xml   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CNNW000020071129e3bt000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal