\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Canada Ponders The Causes And Effects Of Loonie/Greenback Parity   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,170 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2007\par 22:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Lisa Twaronite   \par \par \uc2 SAN FRANCISCO (Dow Jones) -- To Americans who live in northern states, it seems like only yesterday that they had to check their change carefully, to make sure the person at the register didn't try to slip them any of those annoying Canadian quarters.   \par \par \uc2 For one thing, not every U.S. vending machine, coin laundry or parking meter would accept them. For another thing, as recently as 2002, they were worth about 60% less than American quarters, leading to all those "Canadian peso" jokes.   \par \par \uc2 No one is joking now.   \par \par \uc2 This week, for the first time since Jimmy Carter was U.S. president-elect, Canada's dollar traded at parity to its south-of-the-border counterpart.   \par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar -- known colloquially as the "loonie," because of the bird, the common loon, on the coin -- was already on a rising trend on the back of surging commodity prices.   \par \par \uc2 Rising prices tend to lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of commodity-producing countries, and Canada is a major producer of crude oil and metals. Crude futures closed slightly lower Friday, but the new benchmark contract gained almost 5% for the week.   \par \par \uc2 According to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at  \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Canada, Norway and Australia -- all major commodity-producing countries -- have appreciated by 16.5%, 13% and almost 10% respectively against the U.S. dollar this year. Those rises are significantly more than the euro's gain of about 7%.   \par \par \uc2 "The loonie's assent since 2002 has come alongside rising commodity prices, consistent with a solid fundamental basis for the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," wrote Ryan Brecht at Action Economics. "Granted, the most recent run toward parity appears to have a notable speculative component."   \par \par \uc2 The loonie has appreciated against the U.S. dollar despite the fact that interest rate differentials favor the greenback. In general, funds tend to flow out of lower-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as investors seek higher-yielding assets.   \par \par \uc2 The  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  last hiked its benchmark rate a quarter basis point in July to 4.5%, and is expected to hold steady at its next meeting on Oct. 16. That rate remains below the U.S. Federal Reserve's federal funds rate of 4.75%, following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise half-point cut on Tuesday this week.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed's move exacerbated an already weakening dollar, and sent it to its 31-year low against the loonie, a new 15-year low against a basket of six \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and an all-time low against the euro since it began trading in January 1999.   \par \par \uc2 The drop led some strategists to refigure their loonie forecasts.   \par \par \uc2 "We are revising our U.S. dollar/Canadian dollar forecast lower with two quarters below parity. However, the increasingly undervalued status of [the U.S. dollar] raises questions over the sustainability of the downtrend," wrote Matthew Strauss, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 RBC Dominion Securities Inc.\par \par \uc2 "In the absence of improving underlying Canadian dollar fundamentals, we see [the U.S. dollar] returning to above parity by mid-2008," Strauss said.   \par \par \uc2 Canada's economy appeared solid, according to the latest figures. It grew at an annualized 3.4% rate from April to June, above the  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's 2.7% estimate, after growing at a 3.9% rate in the first quarter.   \par \par \uc2 But economists are still gauging whether or not U.S. growth will slow in the coming months, after the meltdown of the subprime mortgage market. The Canadian economy is likely to feel the pain of any U.S. slowdown.   \par \par \uc2 Worryingly, Statistics Canada reported Friday that the country's retail sales fell 0.8% in July from June to about C$34.3 billion, mainly due to a decline in the automotive sector.   \par \par \uc2 Consumers not benefiting   \par \par \uc2 Canadians have not unanimously cheered the loonie's ascent.   \par \par \uc2 Canada's prices have not fallen in line with the loonie's rise, which is normally one of the benefits a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brings to consumers.   \par \par \uc2 Canada's core CPI inflation rate of 2.2%, is within the  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's target range of between 1% and 3%, but it is still the highest of major industrialized economies .   \par \par \uc2 By comparison, U.S. core prices are up 2.1% according the latest data -- the lowest in 17 months and close to the Federal Reserve's target zone.   \par \par \uc2 "Canadian CPI inflation for goods has been running on average 1% below its U.S. counterpart for the past three years. That's a tidy sum for sure, but well below what one may have expected given the average increase of 8% annually in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over that time frame," wrote Doug Porter, deputy chief economist at BMO Capital Markets.   \par \par \uc2 Canadian retail prices "have responded to the loonie's moonshot with all the speed and alacrity of a three-toed sloth on a hot summer's day," he noted.   \par \par \uc2 For example, Porter said, former U.S. Fed chief Alan Greenspan's recently released memoir, "The Age of Turbulence," is now selling for about 26% more in Canada than at U.S. book discounters.   \par \par \uc2 Manufacturers call for cut   \par \par \uc2 A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brings its own set of problems as well.   \par \par \uc2 In an echo of yesteryear, when American manufacturers disparaged the U.S.'s strong dollar policy for making their products more expensive in international markets and leading to layoffs at home, Canada's manufacturers are now urging their \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bring the loonie back down to earth.   \par \par \uc2 On Friday, Canada's largest labor group urged the  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  to cut its key interest rates by half a point in line with the U.S. Fed's move, to prevent layoffs at manufacturers that export to the United States.   \par \par \uc2 "The  \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in financial markets to mitigate the credit crunch," Canadian Labour Congress president Ken Georgetti said in a letter to Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge, which was posted on the labor group's Web site.   \par \par \uc2 "In doing so, it demonstrated the capacity to successfully pursue objectives other than inflation control. The  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  should be as willing to act in response to the manufacturing crisis as it has been in response to the financial crisis," Georgetti said.   \par \par \uc2 Other Canadians acknowledged that loonie's appreciation does have a bright side, in addition to relief from being the butt of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jokes.   \par \par \uc2 "I love the [U.S. dollar/Canadian dollar] at parity. We are all truly richer, as the money we earn and the money we invest is worth more," wrote Sherry Cooper chief economist at BMO Capital Markets.   \par \par \uc2 In a research report published this week, Cooper put into words what some of her fellow Canadians are probably thinking right now.   \par \par \uc2 "The confluence of events of the past two years -- a collapsing housing market stateside and a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  here -- just might mean that the house on the beach with the palm tree out the window may not be as out-of-reach as I thought," she said. [ 09-21-07 1643ET ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020070921e39l0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
