\par\par \b \uc2 BOC'S Dodge: Some Upward Pressure On C$ From Corporate M&A   \b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2007\par 18:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ST. JOHN'S (Dow Jones)--The upward trajectory of the Canadian dollar since the first quarter of 2007 has been harder to explain in terms of historical precedents, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge said Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Dodge stopped short of calling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rise speculatively driven and ruled out direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow its rise.   \par \par \uc2 "Since April, the upward movement in the Canadian dollar is harder to explain on the basis of the kind of historical evidence we've seen," Dodge said in response to an audience question following a speech to a business luncheon in Canada's easternmost city of St. John's, Nfld. [ 06-13-07 1316ET ]   \par \par \uc2 In later remarks to reporters, Dodge expanded on that assessment. He said that the Canadian dollar's appreciation from the end of 2002 up until about April of this year was, in general, solidly fundamentally based on increased demand for Canadian goods and services, such as commodities.   \par \par \uc2 He declined, in response to another question, to term the Canadian dollar's 8% rise since early April as speculative, but he said the move nonetheless has some questionable aspects to it.   \par \par \uc2 "The only comment I'd make is that the movement we've had over this period, on the face of it, appears to be a little more than one might have expected, based on historical relationships," he said.   \par \par \uc2 However, Dodge said "the terms and conditions" that govern when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  would consider direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper a Canadian dollar movement have not been violated.   \par \par \uc2 "We haven't had disorderly markets," Dodge said, referring to conditions that motivated the Reserve Bank of New Zealand's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the rise of the New Zealand dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "Nor we have seen conditions where somehow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  alignments have gotten way out of line," he said. [ 06-13-07 1334ET ]   \par \par \uc2 Dodge acknowledged foreign corporate interests in acquiring Canadian-based companies, primarily resource firms, has had a role in driving the Canadian dollar higher, though he said such influence is "almost inherently impossible to precisely quantify."   \par \par \uc2 "On a net basis, there has probably been some upward pressure," Dodge said. "Some of the upward impact on the Canadian dollar has come from that," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dodge also noted that some of the rise in the Canadian dollar has been a direct result of the downward global pressure on the U.S. dollar in an attempt to diminish global imbalances as represented by the large U.S. current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 Dodge said this process has played out to a degree in the appreciation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Canadian dollar, the euro and other widely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though not yet to the proper extent against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries with large trade and current account surpluses against the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 "What we haven't seen is very much of any U.S. dollar depreciation against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries that are building up big surpluses," he said, citing nations such as China, Japan and some countries in southeast Asians.   \par \par \uc2 "The depreciation hasn't been as big as would be warranted by this global rebalancing," Dodge said, suggesting that "there is more depreciation of the U.S. dollar to come."   \par \par \uc2 Despite recent evidence of a more hawkish Bank of Canada policy bias that has led many financial market players to price in assumptions of at least one Bank of Canada interest rate hike in coming months, Dodge didn't tip his hand as to what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do at its next scheduled interest rate decision on July 10.   \par \par \uc2 He also declined to rule out the possibility that the Bank of Canada might at some point depart from its now customary preference for interest rate adjustments in quarter-percentage-point increments.   \par \par \uc2 "Central banks around the world seem to have gone more to moving in 25-basis-point" increments, Dodge said.   \par \par \uc2 "But there's no rule on the amount of adjustment in any direction," he said.   \par \par \uc2 -By Paul Evans; Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2022; paulr.evans@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (Don Curren in Toronto contributed to this article.) \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 06-13-07 1405ET ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 4219   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020070613e36d000a2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
