\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Canada mulls why C$ so strong, set to raise rates   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Janet Guttsman   \par \uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2007\par 18:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ST. JOHN'S, Newfoundland, June 13 (Reuters) - A booming economy and stronger than expected inflation will prompt higher Canadian interest rates, the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday in an address that pondered, without conclusion, just why the Canadian dollar has soared so high.   \par \par \uc2 Speaking in the Atlantic province of Newfoundland and Labrador, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge admitted the Canadian dollar had risen further and faster than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had expected.   \par \par \uc2 But he said inflationary risks were rising too, pushing inflation above the bank's 2 percent target, and the economy was growing a full percentage point faster than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected.   \par \par \uc2 "At our last fixed announcement date we said that some increase in the target for the overnight rate will be required in the near term to bring inflation back to the target," Dodge said, giving no hint that anything had happened to change that view.   \par \par \uc2 Many economists now expect the  \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  will raise rates on July 10 for the first time in over a year. A quarter-point rise would bring the benchmark overnight rate to 4.5 percent, still well below the corresponding U.S. Fed Funds rate of 5.25 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dodge, who retires in January, said the Canadian dollar's rise since April has been faster than past experience would suggest. But he stopped short of pinning the gains on speculative trading.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 30-year high last week and is up 7 percent since the end of March. On Wednesday afternoon it was at C$1.0661, or 93.80 U.S. cents.   \par \par \uc2 Markets had been watching Dodge's remarks closely for clues to his thinking about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's precipitous rise.   \par \par \uc2 Dodge said there was no cause for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell Canadian dollars to depress the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because factors that would trigger that -- disorderly markets or extreme volatility -- were absent.   \par \par \uc2 He said  \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, posted on its Web site, is different from that of the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which recently sold New Zealand dollars to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower.   \par \par \uc2 But despite his obvious surprise at the domestic dollar's strength, Dodge said the bank did not have enough data yet to conclude whether there was more behind the rise than just strong demand for Canadian commodities and for its goods and services.   \par \par \uc2 "One needs to be very careful not to interpret this day-to-day, week-to-week (Canadian dollar move) as a long-term trend," he said.   \par \par \uc2 He declined to comment on some economists' forecasts that the Canadian dollar would rise to parity with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Some of those same economists are the ones who predicted the Canadian dollar would go to 50 (U.S.) cents, so I think you take those predictions for what they're worth," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The manufacturing sector has complained that the strong dollar makes it hard for firms to compete abroad. Government officials admit that's true, but say it also lowers the cost of machinery and raw materials, which businesses need to be competitive.   \par \par \uc2 Asked if he was worried about the rise in the Canadian dollar, Dodge sounded almost mournful.   \par \par \uc2 "We worry about everything. We're paid to worry at a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he sighed.   \par \par \uc2 CANADA-BANK/DOLLAR (WRAPUP 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|RBN|CANT|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070613e36d001eg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
