\par\par \b \uc2 Some Canadian manufacturers unfazed by C$ parity  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Wright  \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2006\par 22:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TORONTO, May 8 (Reuters) - After being battered for the past four years by a high-flying Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Canada's manufacturers aren't much fazed by the prospect of parity with the U.S. dollar for the first time in three decades.  \par \par \uc2 "The real damage to the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  happened two years ago, when it went from 65 cents (U.S.) to 85 cents. That was a 30 percent jump," said Robert Hattin, president of Edson Machinery in Hamilton, Ontario.  \par \par \uc2 With the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now around 90 U.S. cents, the head of the packaging machine company said: "If it goes to par, it's (only) 10 percent."  \par \par \uc2 Hattin said he expected it would rise to 95 U.S. cents then to parity. He added that those manufacturing companies to survive would be those "who have an attitude to be constantly changing and dealing with issues."  \par \par \uc2 In many cases, manufacturers have managed to adjust to the higher costs and more difficult export climate created by the Canadian dollar's 40 percent rise from record lows.  \par \par \uc2 The sector lost some 71,500 jobs in the last year but it added about 24,500 in April to stir hopes of a rebound.  \par \par \uc2 Hattin said there was little need for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains.  \par \par \uc2 "People who control Canadian companies, especially manufacturing have to become less narrow minded and short-sighted," he said.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor David Dodge was not reluctant to talk down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week when he said its recent rapid gains were a "Type 2" move -- \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  speak for a rise that was not driven by fundamentals and could generate a policy response. The comments briefly tamped down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's headlong rush past 90 U.S. cents.  \par \par \uc2 Dodge also played down predictions from some market analysts that the Canadian dollar could hit parity with the U.S. dollar, a level it has not seen since 1976.  \par \par \uc2 Mel Svendsen, chief executive of Calgary-based auto parts maker Standen's Ltd., said he considered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be the greatest hurdle for his sector to overcome, but he also saw an upside.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a gloomy outlook for manufacturers, but those who do survive will be extremely strong in the future," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar's rise is just one of several headwinds facing the manufacturing sector.  \par \par \uc2 "Manufacturers are kind of caught in the vise right now because they are faced with the high dollar, but some of the reasons the dollar is high are high energy costs, high commodity costs," said Jayson Myers, chief economist at the Canadian Manufacturers & Exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Because Canada is a major oil and gas exporter, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  often pushes higher with the price of crude, which climbed to record highs around $75 in recent weeks, before easing to about $70.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Canada reiterated as recently as Friday that business sectors were adjusting fairly well to both the rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and energy prices, according to deputy governor David Longworth.  \par \par \uc2 The federal agency Export Development Canada, which provides financing and risk management services to companies exporting goods and services or investing in other countries, says on its Web site that "one of every three jobs in Canada depends on export trade.  \par \par \uc2 EDC deputy chief economist, Peter Hall said, "Any economy that has to sustain 35-40 percent (rise) in its key exporting price over such a short amount of time has an enormous amount of hurdles to overcome.  \par \par \uc2 "What's amazing to me as an economic forecaster is that the carnage hasn't been worse than it is," Hall said.  \par \par \uc2 "What's very exciting ... is that we have actually managed to grow exports through this period of time and we're continuing to do that."  \par \par \uc2 ($1=$1.11 Canadian)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CANADA-DOLLAR|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|RBN|CANT|D|M|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060508e258001yw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
