\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-ANALYSIS-Is China heading for G7 clash in London?  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Wheatley, China Economics Editor  \par \uc2 859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2005\par 11:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Clarifies central bankers not attending, paragraphs 17-18)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, June 2 (Reuters) - Who would want to be the Chinese finance minister? Jin Renqing is about to fly halfway round the world for an English breakfast and a polite but firm lecture on the need to scrap the yuan's decade-old peg against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 China's national pride alone guarantees that public arm-twisting at a June 11 meeting in London of finance ministers from the Group of Seven rich democracies would backfire.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing has declared its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime to be a matter of sovereignty and pointedly did not send Jin to the last G7 talks.  \par \par \uc2 Yet even while officials from Premier Wen Jiabao down assert they will not bow to pressure, economists say the diplomatic calendar and more benign financial markets are opening a new window of opportunity to abandon the peg -- if Beijing chooses.  \par \par \uc2 Bill Belchere, chief Asia economist at Macquarie Securities in Hong Kong, said Chinese policy makers recognised that a more market-driven exchange rate would make a lot of sense.  \par \par \uc2 "But this one is going to be more about political concessions that need to be made," Belchere said. "The U.S. has showed its hand and turned this into a very big political issue. So there must be a negotiated agreement on various issues -- a grand political bargain."  \par \par \uc2 BAD LISTENER  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow has given China an ultimatum to reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime or face trade penalties within six months. U.S. law-makers are preparing sanctions of their own if Beijing does not revalue soon.  \par \par \uc2 Stephen Roach, Morgan Stanley's chief economist, said this protectionist pressure had changed the terms of the yuan debate.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking during a recent visit to Beijing, Roach urged China to take action to defuse the threat. The magnitude of any yuan move was less important than the act of changing.  \par \par \uc2 But Roach admitted to worries that China's leaders had backed themselves into a corner. "For a country with an open development model, China has a hard time coping with global feedback," Roach said. "How do you negotiate if you've defined it as a question of national sovereignty?"  \par \par \uc2 The rising political temperature has certainly muddied market thinking. Frank Gong, JP Morgan Chase's chief economist for Asia, says the risks have increased of both a bigger initial revaluation and of no move before the end of June.  \par \par \uc2 Gong says rising U.S. pressure and the imposition of textile curbs last month, just as China seemed to be gearing up for change, had been counterproductive. By the same token, further delay by China would increase the danger of a trade war.  \par \par \uc2 On balance, Gong still sees a significant chance of a shift before President Hu Jintao attends the annual G7/G8 summit in Scotland in early July, while a delay beyond August could sour Hu's planned visit to the United States in September.  \par \par \uc2 "The exact time is hard to predict now since the decision is with Prime Minister Wen himself and it is a one-man show now with all the new political complications," Gong said in a client note.  \par \par \uc2 FEAR OF FLOATING  \par \par \uc2 The economics are also getting more complicated. China's trade surplus is soaring, reinforcing the case for a stronger yuan. Call it a coincidence, but there has been a flurry of comments by influential economists in official media this week reflecting unease that China is relying too much on exports.  \par \par \uc2 Yet does it make sense to throw sand into the export engine when investment growth is slowing, property prices are falling and consumers -- worried about precarious jobs, poor pensions and rising healthcare bills -- are saving rather than spending?  \par \par \uc2 Officials later said that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs were not expected to attend next week's meeting.  \par \par \uc2 But one of the regular G7 club, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge, was in Beijing on Thursday describing Canada's positive experience when it floated its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1950 and then, after eight years of being fixed against the dollar, again in 1970.  \par \par \uc2 On both occasions \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively to preserve a fixed exchange rate, just as China is now. On both occasions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was pleasantly surprised how quickly speculators went away once the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated, Dodge said.  \par \par \uc2 The toughest part, he said, had been to overcome the initial fear of floating. Without giving any direct advice, Dodge's message to Chinese policy makers grappling with the political and economic pros and cons was clear:  \par \par \uc2 "What is perhaps the most important thing is somehow to overcome the fear one has," he said. "One gets used to using controls to maintain the fixed exchange rate, and one really worries that the market system will not somehow be able to handle things when you float and you relax those controls."  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHINA-G7 (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|Z|J|UKI|U|NAT|G|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050602e162001pp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal33