\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Bonds End Mostly Higher In Subdued Trading  \b0\par\par\uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Canadian bonds ended mostly higher in relatively quiet trading on Monday as North American bond markets contended with an array of influences, including concerns about whether the Bank of Japan will continue its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related buying of U.S. Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    3.00s 2005   101.19   unch      2.28% vs 2.28% \par    4.25s 2008   103.99   up 0.09   3.28% vs 3.30% \par    5.25s 2013   107.54   up 0.12   4.25% vs 4.27% \par    5.75s 2029   110.93   up 0.20   4.98% vs 5.00% \par    10-Yr Spread To U.S. 10-Yr: +49 vs +49 \par    CGB JUN 04   112.26  up 0.10  volume 3,393   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Shorter-dated maturities showed little movement on Monday, while longer-dated bonds managed to eke out some modest gains.  \par \par \uc2 Canadian bonds outperformed their U.S. equivalents in most maturities Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "(It's been) fairly quiet. Most of the action is at the longer end of the curve," said David Watt, financial markets economist at BMO Nesbitt Burns Inc.  \par \par \uc2 One factor roiling bond markets on Monday was a press report that the Bank of Japan is considering reducing its large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of March.  \par \par \uc2 Such a move would coincide with the end of Japan's fiscal year and remove a crucial prop from the U.S. Treasurys market.  \par \par \uc2 Watt said the disruptive effect of the report on a potential end to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -driven buying of Treasurys by the BOJ was more of a factor in the U.S. bond market than in Canadian bonds Monday.  \par \par \uc2 But concern about the issue will intensify in coming weeks, he added. "In a couple of weeks and a couple of months, it's going to be something that we're going to be paying a lot more attention to," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Another development on the minds of bond market players on Monday was the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy-setting open market committee meeting on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 While the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to leave its key federal funds target rate at 1.00%, market players will be scrutinizing the accompanying statement for any indication of the timing of interest-rate increases from the Fed.  \par \par \uc2 "The event itself is going to be a nonevent, but people are worried about what the Fed might say in its statement," Watt said. "They have been inching closer and closer to moving to a formal neutral bias."  \par \par \uc2 Data released earlier Monday by the U.S. Treasury Department showed a surge in foreign purchases of U.S. government debt during January. Foreigners recorded $92.0 billion in net purchases of U.S. securities, the biggest monthly buying spree since May 2003, and up from $75.8 billion in December.  \par \par \uc2 Purchases by Japanese investors spiked 31.9%, reflecting massive amounts of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , new motor vehicle sales in Canada totaled a seasonally adjusted 118,779 in January, up 1.3% from 117,211 in December but down 7.1% from 128,838 in January 2003, Statistics Canada said.  \par \par \uc2 The median forecast of analysts surveyed by Thomson Financial/IFR was for new motor vehicle sales to fall 3.6% in January from December.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday morning, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge will speak in Buenos Aires on the topic "Adjusting to Global Economic Change."  \par \par \uc2 The speech bears the same title as an address Dodge gave in Brazil last week which did not directly address the current economic or monetary policy situation, and is not expected to have any direct impact on markets.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Canadian consumer price index data for February will be released. Economists surveyed by Thomson IFR Bonddata expect that overall inflation was at 0.9% on an annual basis in February, while core inflation as defined by the Bank of Canada was 1.2%.  \par \par \uc2 "It's probably going to show that inflation is waning even more in Canada," Watt said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020; don.curren@dowjones.com [ 03-15-04 1600ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1182 4152  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040315e03f00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
