\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  dollar caught by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , bonds down. \b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2002\par 23:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 : \b \highlight22\uc2 CANADA\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPEN 1.5045 PREVIOUS CLOSE 1.5122 - \par \par \uc2  By Ka Yan Ng \par \par \uc2  TORONTO, June 28 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar wilted from a near 17-month high on Friday, caught in the cross-fire of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japan and others in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but analysts said the softness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not likely be sustained. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar on Friday leaped to its highest mark since early February 2001 against the embattled U.S. dollar, at C$1.5028, but failed to end above the psychological 66 U.S. cent level for a second session in a row. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar finished at C$1.5162 to the U.S. dollar, or 65.95 U.S. cents, down modestly from C$1.5122 to the U.S dollar, or 66.13 U.S. cents, at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained steady after news that Canada's economy grew at a very healthy clip in April, but could not sustain the rally as the Bank of Japan sold yen with the help of U.S. and European monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian dollar became a victim of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... That's the main reason why the Canadian dollar lost some ground, but quite frankly, this kind of weakness is temporary," said Benjamin Tal, senior economist at CIBC World Markets. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan sold yen for U.S. dollars with the help of the U.S. Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trying to stem a crisis in confidence in U.S. assets amid accounting woes in Corporate America. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is typically seen as a way of buying time for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has little lasting effect. The medium-term outlook for the Canadian unit is still one of optimism. \par \par \uc2  "At this point, it will be very difficult to change sentiment against the U.S. dollar," added Tal, who sees the Canadian dollar trading around C$1.5050 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, a report showed Canadian growth soared in April as its gross domestic product rose 0.8 percent, its single largest monthly gain since early 2000. \par \par \uc2  April's strong advance in the economy was the seventh straight monthly one, and suggested it could perform better than the U.S. economy in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  The report also signals that the Bank of Canada can continue raising interest rates to cool the red-hot economy. \par \par \uc2  Canada's stronger economy has led the Bank of Canada to raise interest rates twice this year, even as the U.S. Federal Reserve has yet to begin - giving Canada a 75-basis point rate premium over the United States. \par \par \uc2  BONDS DOWN ON CANADIAN GDP \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, domestic bond prices pushed lower on news of Canada's economic strength, despite issues of corporate governance weighing on the minds of investors. \par \par \uc2  Office equipment maker Xerox Corp. was the latest U.S. company to say it had to restate revenues, but U.S. stock markets took the news in stride to seal the first weekly gain for the broad market since mid-May. Toronto stocks also capped a dismal month with an impressive gain on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Still, analysts generally see equities on a negative trend, and government debt could win the day as investors struggle between economic reports and corporate governance. \par \par \uc2  "It's really the U.S. economy against corporate governance. At this point, corporate governance is winning," Tal said. \par \par \uc2  "As long as this is the case, we'll see a flight to safety. What we've seen today is a temporary reaction to the better than expected numbers, but the overall tone is still negative for the equity markets." \par \par \uc2  Canada's two-year bond slipped 10 Canadian cents to C$99.22 to yield 3.926 percent, while the 10-year bond lost 26 Canadian cents to C$103.97 to yield 5.431 percent. \par \par \uc2  The yield spread between the two-year and 10-year bond was 150.5 basis points, compared with 153.1 basis points at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Canada's 30-year bond, due 2029, fell 30 Canadian cents to C$99.15 to yield 5.812 percent. In the United States, the 30-year treasury yielded 5.517 percent. The three-month when-issued T-bill yielded 2.80 percent, up from 2.78 percent at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Canadian financial markets will be closed on Monday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020628dy6s014fp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
