\par\par \b \uc2  Central bankers say economies looking up - but wary. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Janet Guttsman \par \uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2002\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MONTREAL, June 4 (Reuters) - The world's top central bankers offered a cautious assessment of a reviving global economy on Tuesday, but played down the risks of an inflationary spiral that could destroy the recovery. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to a high-ranking group of leading commercial bankers, U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan and his counterparts from Europe, Canada and Japan agreed that things were looking up after a global slowdown deepened by the Sept. 11 attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  But, like any central bankers, they also highlighted uncertainties and risks, including the stubborn need for structural change in Europe and Japan and the probability of a slow recovery in the United States. \par \par \uc2  "It's not going to be a dramatic upswing, but events look increasingly positive," Greenspan told the bankers, who were meeting in Montreal for three days of discussions about the state of the global economy. \par \par \uc2  Noting that neither housing nor consumer spending had dropped during the recent U.S. economic downturn, Greenspan said the upturn was likely to be "pedestrian", rather than the sharp rebounds that followed previous recessions. \par \par \uc2  The panel discussion with central bankers was the focal point of the annual International Monetary Conference of chief executive officers and other top officials from the world's leading financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  Topics ranged from growth and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to what Argentina should do to clamber out of its economic morass - follow the recommendations of the International Monetary Fund, improve banking and bankruptcy legislation, and straighten out messy financial ties between central government and provinces. \par \par \uc2  But the economies of the United States, Japan and the zone of 12 countries using the euro took center stage. \par \par \uc2  "Our expectations for a turnaround in real GDP growth in the euro area has been confirmed by recent published estimates, said European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg, who is responsible for monetary policy in the zone. \par \par \uc2  "Looking ahead, available forecasts all paint a picture of a continued, but slow, strengthening in both foreign and domestic demand." \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg said he expected euro zone inflation to stay around 2 percent for the rest of this year, falling slightly next year. \par \par \uc2  Europe's big economies have by and large underperformed those in north America over recent years, but they remain far stronger than recession-plagued Japan. \par \par \uc2  Yukata Yamaguchi, deputy governor of the Bank of Japan, said a recovery in exports was finally stabilizing economic activity in Japan and industrial output was likely to show a "moderate rise" in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  He called for fiscal restraint to cement a recovery, but conceded it would be difficult to lower taxes substantially at present. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that once the structural headwinds weaken, sometime in the future, then the present monetary policy has the potential to help support sustained economic growth in the future," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, worried that a rise in the yen will choke off its recovery, has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to lower the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But other central banks have stayed on the sidelines, and David Dodge, governor of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , made it clear that he did not think that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worked. \par \par \uc2  "The experience with floating exchange rates, given that there is going to be some volatility, has been an extraordinarily good one, and the best experience is for governments and central banks to keep their hands off," said Dodge, whose own experience of helping the Canadian dollar has been limited to strong words as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled to record lows at the start of this year. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar, bolstered by rising domestic interest rates and a surprisingly strong economy, has recovered well from its January low, shrugging off Sunday's dismissal of Finance Minister Paul Martin, who was credited with eliminating federal budget deficits and winning Canada back its prized Aaa rating from Moody's Investors Service. \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Amran Abocar in Toronto and Tim Ahmann, Joanne Morrison and Glenn Somerville in Washington). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020605dy6500ej1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
