\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Bank of Canada's Dodge says recovery has started. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Atiya Hussain \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2002\par 00:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Adds details, analysts comments, market reaction, changes to Update 2) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 20 (Reuters) - Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge said on Wednesday that both Canada and the global economy seem to have turned the corner, and said modest growth in Canada would pick up sharply in the second half of 2002. \par \par \uc2  But the recovery will be gradual. The Canadian economy may not get back to full speed before 2003, the governor said in a speech before the Canada Society of New York. \par \par \uc2  Dodge, who last month expressed concern about the Canadian dollar's tumble to record lows against the U.S. dollar, did not play up those worries on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "With the improvement since the fall in the geopolitical climate and in consumer confidence in North America and Europe, there are increasing signs that the global economy has turned the corner and will firm as the year progresses," Dodge said. \par \par \uc2  "Still it could be late 2003 before the actual level of output in the Canadian economy will again reach its potential level," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dodge gave no hints about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next interest rate decision, set for March 5. The Bank of Canada has responded aggressively to the global economic downturn, cutting its overnight rate by 3.75 percentage points since 2001 to 2.00 percent in January. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said Dodge's upbeat assessment of the economy suggested the Bank of Canada may be ready to lift rates from the highly stimulative levels they are currently at. \par \par \uc2  While warning that risks to a recovery remain, Dodge stuck to upbeat predictions he made last month, saying the economy will gain momentum from an annualized rate of 1 to 2 percent in the first half of 2002 to around 3 to 4 percent in the second half, and strengthen further in 2003. \par \par \uc2  "The kind of numbers he's talking about at the end of this year are exceptionally strong, signaling that yes, rates will probably have to rise, but they're not in any rush to do so," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at, Bank of Montreal in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  Core inflation, excluding volatile elements like food and energy, should move back up to the government's target level of 2 percent in about two years, Dodge said. Inflation will average just under 1-1/2 percent in the second half of 2002. \par \par \uc2  Bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets showed little reaction to his comments, detecting no shift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy. \par \par \uc2  CANADA MAY SKIRT RECESSION \par \par \uc2  Citing a recent string of positive economic reports, Dodge said the Canadian economy, "may well" show slightly positive growth for the fourth quarter of 2001 and the first quarter of 2002. \par \par \uc2  "Recent economic indicators for Canada support the view that a recovery is starting," he said, pointing to stronger-than-expected household spending, signs of revival in Canadian exports, indications that inventories are being drawn down and a bottoming out in non-energy commodity prices. \par \par \uc2  But Dodge warned that recovery prospects would be hurt if capital investment and business confidence do not pick up as expected this year. \par \par \uc2  "What we see as a significant risk to the outlook of a reasonably strong second half of the year is that investments, particularly investments by large businesses in fixed capital, will in fact not really pick up, and we may have to wait a little longer for that pickup in investment," he said. \par \par \uc2  Canada's economy shrank at an annualized rate of 0.8 percent in third quarter of 2001, and could skirt recession if it ekes out growth in the fourth quarter. That interpretation is based on the simple rule of thumb that defines recession as two consecutive quarters of contraction. The government is due to release Gross Domestic Product data on Feb. 28. \par \par \uc2  Dodge said a flexible exchange rate was important for Canada, but he did not repeat the concern he expressed last month about the Canadian dollar's recent depreciation. \par \par \uc2  "I have no view as to an appropriate level," Dodge said in response to questions, but said the bank's inflation-targetting monetary policy should underpin the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The route to a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is low inflation and high real economic growth," Dodge said. \par \par \uc2  In January, Dodge, along with Canadian Finance Minister Paul Martin, warned that Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall was not helpful for economic confidence in \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's losses a tool in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's arsenal. The Canadian dollar has recovered from record lows hit against the dollar last month. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020220dy2k015o8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
