\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar drops through C$1.60, bonds fall. \b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2002\par 16:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Bank of Canada: CANADA OPEN 1.6023 PREVIOUS CLOSE 1.5978 - \par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Feb 6 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar pushed through the psychologically key C$1.60 level on Wednesday, and domestic bonds fell as high-tech bellwether Cisco Systems forecast better-than-expected earnings. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was at C$1.6022 to the U.S. dollar, or 62.41 U.S. cents, down modestly from C$1.5973 to the U.S. dollar, or 62.61 U.S. cents, at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian dollar has weakened off a little bit, but it's not an aggressive move. The U.S. dollar is doing a little bit better generally. The productivity numbers helped," said Mark Chandler, senior economist at Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on track to a fourth-straight day of declines as the market pushes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to levels seen before Canadian officials began a campaign of talking up the Canadian dollar three weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  On the backburner is risk that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But most observers do not see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming in around C$1.60. Recent data said Canadian foreign reserve holdings stand at US$33.64 billion for January. \par \par \uc2  On the economic front, Canada's help-wanted index, a broad indicator of labor demand, eased slightly in January continuing more than a year of steady decline, while the value of building permits in the country fell 9.7 percent in December from November. \par \par \uc2  In the U.S., a report suggested solid economic recovery as the productivity of U.S. workers grew at a brisker-than-expected pace in the fourth quarter of 2001. \par \par \uc2  The productivity report added little support to bonds, which were more focused on movements in the equity market. \par \par \uc2  CANADIAN BONDS FALL, EYE STOCKS \par \par \uc2  Canadian bonds started in positive territory on Wednesday, but fell after stock markets showed a better tone in the light of Cisco Systems' upbeat forecast. \par \par \uc2  Cisco said it would exceed the current Wall Street consensus estimate for second-quarter earnings per share and revenues. The company will release results after market close on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The two-year bond dipped two Canadian cents to C$103.19 to yield 3.17 percent, while the benchmark 10-year bond shed 12 Canadian cents to C$104.91 to yield 5.323 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The equity market looked like it was in for a bad time, but the Cisco numbers were favorable. That's helped it along somewhat," said Chandler. \par \par \uc2  The 30-year bond, due 2029, dropped 20 Canadian cents at C$101.80 to yield 5.62 percent, while the 30-year U.S. treasury lost 5/32 to yield 5.36 percent. \par \par \uc2  The yield spread between the two-year and 10-year bond moved to 214.7 basis points from 214.5 basis points at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  The three-month when-issued T-bill yielded 1.98 percent, down from 1.99 percent from the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.60 Canadian). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020206dy2600ti7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
