\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar dips, bond rally fizzles. \b0\par\par\uc2 529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2002\par 23:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Bank of Canada: CANADA OPEN 1.5987 PREVIOUS CLOSE 1.5967 - \par \par \uc2  By Ka Yan Ng \par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar edged lower on Tuesday, after splashing about in a modest range, uncertain of what to make of concerns about corporate accounting irregularities and waiting for direction from economic news. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, domestic bonds ended mixed after poor U.S. auction results and news that U.S. lawmakers appeared ready to give up on an economic stimulus package. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar ended at C$1.5973 to the U.S. dollar, or 62.61 U.S. cents, down from C$1.5967, or 62.63 U.S. cents, at the previous close. It was the third straight day the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost ground. \par \par \uc2  "It's been pretty whippy today... but Canada did not move that much today and followed the equity markets, and anticipating some more meaningful data later in the week," said Paul Briggs, trader at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar moved in a C$1.5965 to C$1.6004 range, only briefly popping through the C$1.60 in early dealings. But, it settled around C$1.5965 and C$1.5980 for a large part of the North American session. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian employment report for January will be released on Friday and is expected to show more jobs were lost, but at a slower pace. The unemployment rate is expected to nudge higher to 8.1 percent from 8 percent in December. \par \par \uc2  If it is a weak report, it could add to poor sentiment for North American equities, said analysts. This could also see the Canadian dollar push down through C$1.60 and beyond. \par \par \uc2  On the backburner of many market watchers' minds is the risk that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But most do not see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming in around C$1.60. \par \par \uc2  BONDS END MIXED, TRACK U.S. TREASURIES \par \par \uc2  Canadian bonds started strong on Tuesday, fell into negative territory, but ended almost flat by session's end. \par \par \uc2  The two-year bond was unchanged at C$103.21 to yield 3.162 percent, while the benchmark 10-year bond was down 5 Canadian cents to C$105 to yield 5.31 percent. \par \par \uc2  Stocks fell sharply after Washington said its economic stimulus legislation is all but dead, dealing another blow to the market already stung by doubts about accounting standards. \par \par \uc2  "The flight-to-quality theme was challenged in the second half of the session when stocks began to rebound," said one analyst. "But, there was no follow-through rally in the stock market as accounting jitters remain a chief concern." \par \par \uc2  The 30-year bond, due 2029, was unchanged at C$102 to yield 5.605 percent, while the 30-year U.S. treasury rose 5/32 to yield 5.339 percent. \par \par \uc2  The yield spread between the two-year and 10-year bond moved to 214.5 basis points from 213.8 basis points at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  The three-month when-issued T-bill yielded 1.99 percent, up from 1.98 percent from the previous close. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020205dy2500wes\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
