\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Finance Min Martin Talks Down US Dollar Adoption \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Esterl \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2002\par 22:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Many business leaders north of the  49th parallel think their government should consider junking the long-ailing Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and hooking up with the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  But they won't find an ally in Finance Minister Paul Martin, who's been actively talking up the Canadian dollar in recent days after it sank to a new historic low around 62  U.S. cents in mid-January. \par \par \uc2  "We have every advantage in maintaining our own monetary policy," Martin told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview here at the World Economic Forum. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar, which was worth more than the greenback a few decades ago, has lost around 30% of its relative value in the past 10 years. The slide seemed to build up speed after the country's mint introduced a dollar coin bearing a rendering of a loon, a local bird celebrated for its diving prowess. \par \par \uc2  A recent poll by Compas Inc. showed more than half of Canadian business leaders think the country should weigh adopting the U.S. dollar, and 55% are in favor of a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  After years of implicit suggestions out of Ottawa that a  depreciating Canadian dollar was good for the export-oriented economy, Martin and Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge have fought back in recent days with a spirited verbal campaign. \par \par \uc2  Last week, Martin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued and blamed its woes on a "perception problem." And on Thursday, Dodge told investors in New York that the Canadian  dollar's recent depreciation is hurting consumer and business confidence and is "clearly not helpful" for the economy. \par \par \uc2  That aggressive talk has had an effect, creating a floor  of sorts for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was trading Friday afternoon at C$1.5907-17 to the U.S. dollar, a little under 63 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  Martin said on Friday that monetary officials aren't targeting any trading range for the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It's very important to be clear the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central) bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has  said, and we share that view, that we don't talk about a level. Our comments were aimed at the direction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he told Dow Jones. \par \par \uc2  He said that Canada's "underlying" economic trends - including a declining debt load, a current-account surplus and a tentative recovery in domestic demand - were "not reflected" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  "We are very well-positioned for recovery," he added, pointing out healthy growth in housing statistics. \par \par \uc2  Canada reported Thursday that the economy edged 0.2% higher in November from October. It advanced 0.3% in October, the first positive reading since May. \par \par \uc2  Martin said he "fully expects" the country's ratio of debt to gross domestic product to decline further this year.  The government is aiming to cut the ratio to 40% in the next four years from the current 50%, with an "ultimate target" of 25%. \par \par \uc2  Monetary policy officials haven't said what they'll do if the loonie dips again in the coming days or weeks. \par \par \uc2  But Marc Chandler, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at HSBC in New York, predicted in a report Friday that "the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  would 1) likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but not 2) raise rates to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 " if the U.S. dollar returns to the highs seen earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Chandler, who met with Martin and Dodge, is forecasting that the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be trading in a broad range of C$1.58-1.61 to the U.S. dollar in the near term, but didn't rule out new pressure down the road. \par \par \uc2  -By Mike Esterl, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4026 mike.esterl@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020201dy21001aj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
