\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian bonds close higher in flight to safety. \b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2002\par 22:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Jan 29 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds rallied to a higher close on Tuesday as investors fled equity markets and sought the safe haven of government securities. \par \par \uc2  North American stock markets fell sharply in late afternoon action as investors fretted about corporate profits and the soundness of corporate financial statements in the wake of the Enron debacle. \par \par \uc2  Canada's two-year bond jumped 18 Canadian cents to C$103.23 to yield 3.169 percent, while the 10-year benchmark bond rose 46 Canadian cents to C$104.48 to yield 5.382 percent. \par \par \uc2  "It started with the equity mess today," said Hank Cunningham, director of fixed income at Correspondent Network. \par \par \uc2  "I think the bond market actually was a little bit oversold too so it didn't take much of a spark. It's an FTQ day: flight to quality." \par \par \uc2  The Toronto Stock Exchange's main index finished down 77 points, while the Dow Jones industrial average slumped 240 points and the Nasdaq composite fell 51 points. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the session, bonds got off to a weak start in the wake of sturdy U.S. economic numbers, but spillover sentiment from a resurgent Canadian dollar helped cap losses. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge renewed his verbal support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, expressing his concern about the weak dollar in speeches in Western \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday and Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dodge reiterated that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still a tool in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's arsenal but he would not elaborate on what circumstances it would take for the bank to move to support the faltering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dodge's comments sent the Canadian dollar surging to one-month highs against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though it came off those highs through the session. \par \par \uc2  "If the Canadian dollar can regain a firming trend, given that spreads to the U.S. are comfortable, it could see international investors looking at Canada more favorably," said Dawn Desjardins, bond strategist at J.P. Morgan Canada. \par \par \uc2  "That would be good news for the bonds and for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Also released on Tuesday were U.S. data that showed consumer confidence rose for the second straight month in January, while durable goods orders also came in higher. \par \par \uc2  That reinforced the market's view that the U.S. Federal Reserve's easing cycle is likely over but that interest rates will remain low for several months. \par \par \uc2  The vast majority of Wall Street bond dealers believe the Fed is done cutting the overnight rate, but will not begin boosting interest rates again until at least the second half of the year, according to a recent Reuters poll. \par \par \uc2  "They've already built in no rate change into the marketplace so I don't think that will cause any action," Cunningham said. "If (the Fed) decides to go for an insurance cut, that would cause some interesting action, that's for sure." \par \par \uc2  The Fed's policy-setting arm started its two-day meeting on Tuesday and is expected to keep rates steady at the conclusion of its meeting on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  On the Canadian data front, the December composite index rose a better than expected 0.4 percent in December, topping forecasts for an 0.2 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  The 30-year bond, due 2029, rose 45 Canadian cents to C$101.20 to yield 5.663 percent while the U.S. 30-year treasury gained a full point to yield 5.401 percent. The yield spread between the two-year and 10-year bond moved to 221.2 basis points from 216.5 basis points at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  The three-month when-issued T-bill yielded 1.98 percent, up from 1.97 percent at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.59 Canadian). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020129dy1t017zk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
