\par\par \b \uc2  SPECIAL REPORT-Weak Canadian dollar leaves markets guessing. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Amran Abocar \par \uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2002\par 17:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Jan 29 (Reuters) - A new deluge of supportive words has stemmed the Canadian dollar's tumble to new lows, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still fragile and another plunge could leave the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  few options. \par \par \uc2  Officials have so far ducked the question of whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  will add the big gun of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar's defense after the small-arms fire of verbal support, but the markets seem determined to test the bank's resolve. \par \par \uc2  They are looking to see if the bank will walk the walk - purchase Canadian dollars on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up - as well as talk the talk. \par \par \uc2  "It's the old story of the boy who cried wolf," said Andy Busch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor at Bank of Montreal in Chicago. "It's really a question of 'show me some action'. If you're concerned about it, fine, we understand that. We know you don't like it. But do something about it." \par \par \uc2  Canada's prime minister, finance minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor last week shed a long history of indifference toward the dollar, loudly lamenting its decline to a record low of C$1.6194 to the U.S. dollar, or 61.75 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  They all said the dollar should be higher, based on the Canadian economy's solid fundamentals - a large current account surplus, a budget surplus and falling government debt. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor David Dodge went so far as to chide markets for ignoring \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong fundamentals. He said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when central banks buy their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the price up, was "a tool in our tool chest." \par \par \uc2  The officials renewed their offensive this week, launching a second barrage of supportive comments that helped push the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to one-month highs against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  But the tough talk hardly has dealers quaking in their shoes. \par \par \uc2  WORDS ARE NOT ENOUGH \par \par \uc2  "My experiences over the years is that politicians are best not talking about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the market likes to take them on," said Stephen Wateridge, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at CIBC World Markets, who does not expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Once everyone starts throwing their 10 cents in about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think it generally leads to an adverse reaction." \par \par \uc2  While the market may be calling the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bluff on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank of Montreal's Busch said there was still some uncertainty about what the bank's next step might be, given its relationship with the government. \par \par \uc2  "They're much more politically biased than other central banks," he said. "Even the Japanese have now a degree of independence from the elected government, which is a good thing. "It depends on how much pressure (Prime Minister Jean) Chretien wants to put on Dodge." \par \par \uc2  Sharing the view of several other dealers, Busch said he did not expect the bank to defend the dollar unless it dropped to C$1.65 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say there is little the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do to stop the fall. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a short-term cure that could escalate into a game of chicken as the market eyes Canada's $34 billion in foreign reserves and bets that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action won't go on indefinitely. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, raising interest rates just as Canada struggles out of recession would be "economic suicide," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  That leaves the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a less palatable choice: let the market do what it will with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and wait for the dollar to recover once the economy rebounds. \par \par \uc2  At that point, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a small scale and give a tide of positive sentiment toward the Canadian dollar a push. Battling against a market sour on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is futile, according to senior economist Benjamin Tal of Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce. \par \par \uc2  Tal said the market is more interested in sentiment than fundamentals and in Canada's short-term economic prospects in relation to its biggest trading partner, the United States. \par \par \uc2  "That's the only thing on the investors' mind...so let's leave the fundamentals to the academics," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has fallen on the view that Canada will lag a U.S. recovery, and because the U.S. dollar is strong. \par \par \uc2  Tal said the verbal defense is preferable to actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it keeps the market guessing. \par \par \uc2  "No \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is better than a failed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. "You use your last bullet by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then you're really powerless against the market, which will continue to test you." \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.59 Canadian). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020129dy1t00wku\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
