\par\par \b \uc2  Bk Of Cda's Dodge: F/X  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Still In Toolbox \b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2002\par 20:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones News Service \par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WINNIPEG -(Dow Jones)- Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor David Dodge continued his cautious talking up of the Canadian dollar Monday, telling reporters here that the Canadian unit's recent slump to all-time lows is "not helpful" for the Canadian economy and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still considers possible direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as a tool in its arsenal. \par \par \uc2  In a news conference after a luncheon speech in Winnigeg, Dodge didn'ot go substantially beyond comments originally made last week following the release of the latest update to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy report. \par \par \uc2  But he stressed again that the declining Canadian dollar could pose a potential confidence issue for the Canadian economy. \par \par \uc2  "As I've said before, the current recent depreciation of the Canadian dollar is certainly not helpful to economic confidence or the process of adjustment here in Canada," Dodge said. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar in recent weeks has sunk to new all-time lows near the level of C$1.6200 for one U.S. dollar, raising calls from some quarters of the Canadian financial community that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should dip into its estimated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than C$34 billion to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Canadian dollar's support. \par \par \uc2  However, when pressed several times to explain the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's views on direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the sagging Canadian dollar, Dodge declined to elaborate on statements last week that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an option for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tool stays in the tool box," Dodge said. "Because it's in the toolbox, that's where, in a sense, it stays, and you wouldn't expect me to comment on when we might take it out of the tool box, if we might take it out of the tool box, and how many of those tools we might use." \par \par \uc2  Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge said there are several factors already in place - notably a pick-up in world demand for Canadian exports and rising foreign and domestic investment - pointing to an eventual rebound for the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly, for an economy that relies on trade as much as we do, strengthening world demand is very important," Dodge said. "That factor is there, already." \par \par \uc2  Dodge said that investment will likely pick up somewhat more slowly as this influence on the Canadian dollar's value has been hurt by disappointing earnings results for both domestic and foreign firms. \par \par \uc2  Dodge also said that, as the impacts of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks continue to modify risk assessments and investment behavior of domestic corporations, this could affect the Canadian economy's level of potential output in 2002. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada has in recent years estimated that the Canadian economy's level of potential annual output growth is about 3.0%. However, under the influence of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks, Dodge said the bank's assessments are pointing to a slight lowering of this potential growth rate in 2002. \par \par \uc2  "Our thinking is that during the course of 2002, some of these adjustments will be made, and that probably will mean that the rate of output growth and potential could be a bit less," Dodge said. \par \par \uc2  "But it's a one-time effect that won't last long, and will likely be measured in quarters," he said. \par \par \uc2  -Paul Evans, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2022; paulr.evans@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  4236 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020020128dy1s001si\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
