\par\par \b \uc2  Bk Of Canada Offers Rosy Outlook, But Talks Tough On C$ \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Evans \par \uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2002\par 21:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TORONTO -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of Canada continued to hammer home an optimistic view of Canada's 2002-03 economic prospects on Wednesday, and in the process, appeared to be telling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that it's time to stop hammering the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Though it slightly pared back some earlier growth estimates and forecast a "somewhat greater" degree of economic slack in 2002, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first-quarter update to its November monetary policy report (MPR) offered a generally benign view of the way forward in which economic growth steadily gains momentum and culminates in above-potential growth of between 3% to 4% in the second half of the calendar year. \par \par \uc2  The inflation outlook, and by extension the monetary policy outlook, was similarly rosy, with the Bank foreseeing yearly core inflation rates below 1.5% in the second half, and all-inclusive inflation rates lower still until late in the year. \par \par \uc2  The MPR update's economic forecasts had been largely foreshadowed in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's limited quarter-percentage point interest rate ease and accompanying commentary last week. \par \par \uc2  It struck many analysts as reasonable given recent domestic data and global economic and geo-political developments, even though financial markets have likely exaggerated the extent of Bank of Canada optimism by pricing in a return to monetary tightening as early as the beginning of the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  "I take away a signal that interest rate easing is pretty much done, but also a sense that there won't be any tightening for quite a long time," said Andrew Spence, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and credit market research at TD Securities in Toronto. "That's a world I can live with under the circumstances - I wouldn't disagree with the Bank's assessments." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    End Of Official BenignNeglect Of C$ May Be At Hand\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of considerably more interest was Bank of Canada governor David\par Dodge's subsequent commentary on the Canadian dollar, which many saw as\par finally offering signs of a break with the recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy of\par discussing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only in vague, anodyne terms.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Though he predictably refused to be drawn into discussions of specific\par levels or tactical prescriptions for reviving the ailing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Dodge\par stressed repeatedly at a post-MPR press conference that the Canadian\par dollar's recent slump to new historical lows is "not helpful" and raises\par confidence issues for the Canadian economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dodge also echoed Monday's remark by Canadian finance minister Paul\par Martin that the Canadian dollar is suffering from a perception problem\par and that its value doesn't reflect the improving fundamentals of the\par Canadian economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank, Dodge said, is "closely monitoring" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par developments.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As RBC Capital Markets chief economist of the Americas John Johnston\par pointed out, "in G7-speak this is usually seen as a threat of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While active Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par considered unlikely under current conditions, even such tempered remarks\par are seen by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers as long-overdue signs that an implicit\par policy of benign official neglect of the Canadian dollar may be over.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Steve Demers, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at National Bank in\par Montreal, termed this week's remarks by Dodge and Martin as an\par "important first step in the right direction" that could have positive\par implications over the long-term for the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "One of the key factors negatively impacting the Canadian dollar since\par at least 1998 has been this belief that the Canadian authorities are\par comfortable with their weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , or even that they have a weak\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy," Demers said. "With the Canadian dollar being so weak\par recently, it's very important for them to state that this isn't their\par policy - we had a tiny bit of that from Mr. Martin, and Mr. Dodge made\par it much more clear today."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Active Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in defense of the Canadian dollar\par still isn't considered likely unless the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  starts to crumble\par rapidly and in a volatile fashion under the pressure of a speculative\par assault, or if renewed weakness spills over to disrupt activity in\par Canadian fixed-income and equity markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And Dodge himself appeared to extend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market a\par period of grace in which to adjust to Canada's improving fundamentals\par with his acknowledgement that financial markets always find it difficult\par to deal with turning points in the economy, such as the one the Bank\par believes is in progress now.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But to many observers, the underlying message of Dodge's remarks is\par that the Bank of Canada will no longer be as tolerant as it has been of\par a Canadian dollar whose weakness violates any conception of fundamental\par value.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What was telling is that the Bank of Canada views what has happened\par to the exchange rate as not being fundamentally driven," said Spence of\par TD Securities. "So we've got to think  that Dodge is signalling that the\par authorities are prepared to take some action with respect to the\par exchange rate if this goes on - that seems to me to be the implicit\par suggestion."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar distanced itself further from its C$1.6194 record\par intraday low in the wake of Dodge's remarks, moving to the C$1.6025 by\par late afternoon Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - By Paul Evans, Dow Jones Newswires; (416) 306-2022\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par paulr.evans@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020123dy1n00b41\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
