\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of Canada Policy Update Seen Reticent On C$ Slide \b0\par\par\uc2  By Don Curren \par \uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2002\par 19:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TORONTO -(Dow Jones)- Canadian dollar watchers hoping for a clear response to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent plunge in the Bank of Canada's monetary policy update Wednesday will likely be disappointed, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  While the Canadian dollar's recent dive deeper into record low territory has heightened expectations for the update, both the timing of its preparation before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest slump and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's typical reticence on the subject may preclude any lengthy discussion of it, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge will likely address the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline in his news conference after the report is released, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I would be surprised if it was emphasized in the report, but I would be equally surprised if it didn't come up as the main topic in the press conference after," said Mark Chandler, senior economist at Goldman Sachs Canada. \par \par \uc2  The report will be released at 10:30 a.m. EST (1530 GMT), with the news conference following at 11:15 a.m. EST. (1615 GMT) \par \par \uc2  The report will only include information up to the bank's last interest-rate adjustment on Jan. 15, before the Canadian dollar's most recent plunge into record-low territory. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar, which  closed at C$1.5888 on Jan. 15, surged to an all-time high of C$1.6194 on Monday. The Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered somewhat on Tuesday, and was hovering around C$1.6075 in early afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  The scheduling of the update means that anybody looking for a "big, lengthy discussion" on the dollar is going to be disappointed, said Doug Porter, senior economist at BMO Nesbitt Burns Inc. in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  The fact that the last monetary policy report in November contained little discussion of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also suggests that any comments on it will be limited, he added. \par \par \uc2  "They really avoided the topic in the November report, so I'd be surprised if there's anything really illuminating in the update on the dollar front," Porter said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Martin's Remarks Heighten Expectations\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian Finance Minister Paul Martin's supportive comments on the\par Canadian dollar on Monday have fostered speculation Dodge may make some\par strong statements of his own at the news conference on Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a conference call to reporters, Martin said the Canadian dollar is\par undervalued and suffering from a "perception problem."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market players credited Martin's comments with contributing to the\par Canadian dollar's modest recovery on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Martin's comments have also fueled conjecture that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets on behalf of the beleaguered\par Canadian dollar, another subject Dodge is likely to be asked about at\par the news conference.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts said they expected the economic outlook in the update to be\par broadly consistent with the forecast in the November report, which\par called for 1.5% gross domestic product growth in both 2001 and 2002.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think it will just firm up the economic outlook they had in the\par first place," Porter said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the report emphasized the economic uncertainties following the\par Sept. 11 terrorist attacks on the U.S. and pointed out its forecast was\par based on a number "working assumptions," the update may indicate that\par the level of uncertainty has diminished, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at the Bank of Montreal, said\par the update's economic forecast could be slightly more upbeat than the\par November report\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ferley said the bank may suggest some upside risk to its forecasts of\par 1.5% growth in 2002, and point out that 2001 appears to have ended on a\par stronger note than earlier anticipated.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The update may address the question of the Canadian dollar's decline\par by emphasizing that the economic data has improved recently, and\par implying that will provide support to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Ferley said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the policy update is unlikely to have a significant impact on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think it would be viewed as a success if it could stabilize the\par Canadian dollar," Ferley said. "Sentiment still seems very negative on\par the Canada, almost in total disregard to the economic numbers."\par    -By Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020;\par don.curren@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020122dy1m008n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
