\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar fights back as market heeds Martin. \b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2002\par 13:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jun 22 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar clawed back some ground on Tuesday from recent record lows against its U.S. counterpart after Canada's finance minister said late in the previous session he was worried about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness. Finance Minister Paul Martin said on Monday the country was "not happy" with the low level of the Canadian dollar but that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would eventually reflect the country's good economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  His warning followed the Canadian dollar sliding more than three Canadian cents in recent sessions, hitting an all-time low just shy of C$1.62 per greenback on Monday, as investors bet on a quicker U.S. recovery and speculation of negative merger and acquisition flows. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/Canada's come off a bit after we had those overnight comments by Martin," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  At 1134 GMT, the Canadian dollar was trading around C$1.6090 , about a cent stronger than the record lows set on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Martin's comments had put them on alert for any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  though they added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to step in unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline became less orderly. \par \par \uc2  Attention was therefore focused on Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge's Monetary Policy Report Update on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders paid little attention to news that Canadian consumer prices rose 0.1 percent on the month in December and 0.7 percent on the year. Analysts polled by Reuters had expected prices to fall 0.1 percent on the month. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Australian dollar was steady in Europe after rallying in Asia, once again flirting with the US$0.52 level , as large domestic and Japanese funds found a renewed appetite for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as growth hopes revived the world over. \par \par \uc2  New Zealand's dollar was trading at US$0.43 , little changed from levels seen in Asian trading when it had rallied nearly half a cent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020122dy1m00ov4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
