\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Dollar's Latest Debacle Stirs  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Talk \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Evans \par \uc2 1,020 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2002\par 20:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TORONTO -(Dow Jones)- The plight of the battered Canadian dollar has become so grave as to raise calls from some quarters for Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  A few large U.S. dollar orders from fund accounts and follow-up stop-loss selling propelled the Canadian dollar through a generally illiquid market to a new historical low of C$1.6170 to a U.S. dollar overnight, extending a three-month spiral of futility for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  And while \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a weapon that has been gathering dust in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's arsenal since it was last used in August 1998, the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sudden and rapid plunge had some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers and market players waiting in vain for signs of Bank of Canada activity entering Thursday's North American session. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has been wallowing in record low territory ever since it first fell through its previous C$1.5850 all-time low in late November, but the speed and extent of its latest plunge has convinced some that its weakness is no longer a credible reflection of Canadian economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Senior economist David Rosenberg of Merrill Lynch Canada in Toronto said that while it's natural to expect a weaker Canadian dollar in a period of North American recession and slumping global growth, recent activity has seen a troubling delinking of the Canadian dollar from the behaviour of other global asset classes. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian dollar stands out more than any other  piece of paper on the planet in terms of still pricing in a global recession - every other security has moved on," he said. "But the Canadian dollar hasn't, and if it's broke then it's time to fix it - I think we've crossed that line in the past day or so." \par \par \uc2  Over the past month or so, said Rosenberg, other value indices that, like the Canadian dollar, are sensitive to global growth expectations  - such as gold prices, yield spreads between government and higher-risk corporate bonds, and commodity prices, to name a few - have all strengthened. \par \par \uc2  Yet the Canadian dollar's relentless weakening has suddenly accelerated, a development that Rosenberg says "breaches any semblance of fundamental value - the Canadian dollar's got no business being down here." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Widespread Bemusement As To Reasons For Latest Plunge\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It's a telling indication of how baffling the Canadian dollar's\par underperformance has become to the experts to see the growing list of\par factors advanced to explain its latest midadventure.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Merrill Lynch's Rosenberg earlier Thursday put out a top-10 list of\par reasons covering everything from expectations of poor fourth quarter\par 2001 earnings for Canadian corporations to delayed adverse reactions to\par a spike in Canada's unemployment rate and Tuesday's 25 basis point Bank\par of Canada cut in short term interest rates, a move that some suggest\par didn't go far enough for the sputtering Canadian economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers cited factors as diverse as Japanese fund\par repatriation ahead of the March 31 Japanese fiscal year-end, and\par disappointment over recent developments in Canadian federal politics.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Whatever the reasons, Rosenberg said it's time for the Bank of Canada\par to inject a dose of clarity by dipping into the record $34.2 billion in\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves that Canada has built through much of the\par 1990s to at least steady, if not reverse, the Canadian dollar's course.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Now that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's been out of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  game for\par so long, it's no longer a standard procedure and would likely carry some\par clout because of its rarity," said Rosenberg. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  right now\par would be a pretty big stick - use it!"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Though most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers are taking the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at its\par word that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will only come as a result of a volatile,\par disorderly, or panic-driven assault on the Canadian dollar, there is\par some sympathy for Rosenberg's contention that official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on\par the Canadian dollar's behalf is now necessary to stave off a calamitous\par plummet.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some have argued that the abandonment in September 1998 of symmetrical\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tactics by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and only tepid supportive\par remarks on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since then by government and Bank of Canada\par officials alike, amounts to a policy of official benign neglect of the\par Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Unless government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy-makers begin talking up\par their desire for a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to this view, the\par Canadian dollar has no hope of halting the long-term downtrend that\par began in late 1991, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trading at around C$1.1200.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It depends on who talks it up - it would be setting a precedent if\par (Canadian finance minister) Martin or (Prime Minister) Chretien were to\par talk it up - that would probably have a positive impact," said Steve\par Demers, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at National Bank in Montreal. "If\par (Bank of Canada governor) David Dodge makes a vague statement, it would\par help the Canadian dollar in the short-term as a first step, if the\par second step were to be a clear \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, Demers said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely unless\par the Canadian dollar falls rapidly to about the C$1.6350 area over the\par next few days.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We would need to be in an extreme situation where the Canadian dollar\par has appreciated very quickly, it's a one-way street, and speculators are\par dominating the market," Demers said. "There's no risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par until C$1.6350 for now, but if we move up there we need to be careful -\par the Bank has double the amount of foreign reserves it had three years\par ago, and if it could put through about $10 billion, it would have a\par significant impact in the short-term."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar steadied somewhat after its overnight descent, and\par by mid-day in Toronto and New York had returned to trade around\par C$1.6100.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - By Paul Evans, Dow Jones Newswires; (416) 306-2022\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par paulr.evans@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020118dy1h000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
