\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Europe seen pushing exchange rates up G7 agenda. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2001\par 15:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 30 (Reuters) - Exchange rates are set to rise up the Group of Seven nations' agenda as European policymakers indicate the euro is close to the bottom of their preferred trading range just days before G7 officials meet. \par \par \uc2  French Finance Minister Laurent Fabius on Tuesday pinpointed $0.95 as a reasonable exchange rate for the euro, which is currently three cents weaker, and said economic fundamentals should allow a move towards dollar parity. \par \par \uc2  His comments come a day after Heribert Zitzelsberger, a German deputy finance minister, said rates between $0.90 and $1.00 represented a "healthy middle ground" which was acceptable to exporters and would also help contain inflation pressures. \par \par \uc2  Their comments have got analysts expecting exchange rates to feature at Thursday's gathering of G7 deputy finance ministers, which will lay the groundwork for the meeting their chiefs will hold later in February. \par \par \uc2  "The Europeans will be somewhat disappointed by the euro's performance in the past fortnight and if it goes below $0.90 they will want to revive the spirit of G7 cooperation," said Jim O'Neill, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  "It is frequently the deputies that agree all the nitty gritty details so these meetings can be important." \par \par \uc2  STOPPING RETREAT TURNING INTO ROUT \par \par \uc2  The G7, which comprises the United States, Japan, Germany, France, Italy, Britain, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly to buy euros in September 2000 and expressed shared concern about the potential implications of its weakness for the world economy. \par \par \uc2  While the euro went on to plumb record lows in the months that followed, repeated rounds of solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and mounting evidence of U.S. economic slowdown succeeded in boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of 2000. \par \par \uc2  But investors' enthusiasm has begun to flag in recent weeks, mainly on growing confidence that the U.S. Federal Reserve is acting swiftly enough to prevent a protracted slump which has revived demand for U.S. stocks and dollars. \par \par \uc2  This has meant the euro never got beyond $0.96 in the first week of January before turning tail. It is now nearly eight cents away from the dollar parity rate which France's Fabius said on January 14 it would be scaling within weeks. \par \par \uc2  European officials' comments this week suggested they would be keen to stop the euro's retreat before it turned into a rout, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The euro failed to grasp the chance to get to parity and the risk now is that it will go back below $0.90," said Colin Clarkson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Sumitomo Bank. \par \par \uc2  "If we fall much below there, it will get people thinking about the record lows so I would expect rhetoric from both Europe and the G7 and people will probably start muttering about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if we break below $0.85." \par \par \uc2  WHAT IS UPPER LIMIT TO EURO'S COMFORT ZONE? \par \par \uc2  But if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are now keeping a watchful eye on forthcoming G7 meetings, some investors were more concerned by Zitzelsberger's suggestion that dollar parity might mark the upper boundary of Germany's preferred range for the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The market's perception had been that parity was not a boundary and so I am slightly disquieted by the comments," said Tim O'Dell, fund manager at Investec Asset Management in London. \par \par \uc2  He said his firm had bought euros at the end of 2000 and already taken profits in the first month of 2001. He said his firm was on the lookout for opportunities to buy the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but added: \par \par \uc2  "If, on the euro's next upleg, we saw more such comments about parity, it would be a very powerful impediment to the euro's progress." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx1u07bn9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
