\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian role in joint euro defense seen as "token". \b0\par\par\uc2  By Don Curren \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2000\par 21:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Figures in U.S. dollars unless noted) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Canada made only a "token" contribution to the joint defence of the euro last month and may not join further actions on behalf of the embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Official international reserve information for September released on Wednesday revealed Canada contributed only $97 million to the joint defence of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on September 22. \par \par \uc2  With the total \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from central banks amounting to an estimated $5 billion to $6 billion, Canada's contribution was little more than a symbolic gesture, some analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I would actually classify that kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as token," said Doug Porter, senior economist at BMO Nesbitt Burns Inc. \par \par \uc2  The $97 million revealed in the monthly release on Canada's international reserves was smaller than the $140 million estimated by some analysts after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  originally occurred, he said. "It's a little less than expected, but it's in the ballpark," Porter said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada's participation in the joint rescue for the beleaguered euro seems to have been triggered by a desire to show solidarity with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other G7 monetary authorities, said Porter and other analysts. \par \par \uc2  "I think everyone was a bit taken by surprise by that decision. You could make the case that they were probably asked directly by the ECB or by the Europeans, and being good global citizens, they did see it as one of the most pressing concerns for the global economy and did join in the effort," Porter said. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has put in a wobbly performance since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - it dropped 245 points from its close of C$1.4865 (67.27 U.S. cents) on September 21 to its low of C$1.5110 (66.18 cents) on Monday before recovering to C$1.4966 (66.82 U.S. cents) on Wednesday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  While analysts have pointed to a variety of factors for the decline, the weakness may give the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  grounds to hesitate before joining another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Porter said. \par \par \uc2  "I think it was a show of solidarity to support the euro, but given how the Canadian dollar has fared since that first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I suspect they won't jump in willingly, again," he said. \par \par \uc2  Porter also said some observers were raising questions about why the bank used Canadian dollars to buy euros instead of drawing on its reserves of U.S. dollars. "I think a lot of people still scratching their heads over why they used Canadian dollars, and just didn't buy the euros with some of their U.S. dollar holdings," he said. \par \par \uc2  The decision to use Canadian dollars might have been prompted by a desire to make a broad-based statement of support for the euro, but that could be accomplished as well by selling U.S. dollars, Porter said. \par \par \uc2  Jeoffrey Hall, managing analyst at Thomson IFR Bonds, said the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's involvement in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "a little more than a token gesture." \par \par \uc2  "It seems small to me. Certainly, if it were done alone, it would have been laughed at," he added. \par \par \uc2  But Canada has economic justifications for supporting the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added. "Canada has a vested interest in this because Europe is a major and growing partner of Canada, and some of the value added that Canada exports to the U.S. is ultimately bound for the European Union, as well," he said. \par \par \uc2  "It's not surprising to have found \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whether they're a G7 nation or not," Hall said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted selling of the Canadian dollar against the euro, perhaps correcting some overvaluation of the Canadian unit against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. "It rebalanced the global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  framework, a little bit," he said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it did great damage to the Canadian dollar with respect to the U.S. dollar, either," he added. \par \par \uc2  Further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will depend on the level at which euro trades in coming weeks, Hall said. \par \par \uc2  Porter said that further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think any of the central banks realistically expected that one small round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was going to be the final word on the subject," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Meaningful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a long process. It's a war, not a battle. I think we're bound to see more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the weeks ahead," Porter said. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.49 Canadian). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwa405hd4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
